\par\par \b \uc2 China Yuan Down Late As  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\uc2  Boosts Dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 11:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                   Vs Parity    Pvs \par USD/CNY Central Parity   6.4386               6.4441 \par USD/CNY OTC 0830 GMT     6.4390    +0.01%     6.4341 \par              High        6.4491    +0.16% \par              Low         6.4344    -0.07% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SHANGHAI (Dow Jones)--China's yuan was lower against the dollar late Thursday, as the U.S. unit rose in Asia after the Japanese government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 But traders said they expect the Chinese authorities will continue to let the yuan rise in the short term in the face of stubbornly-high inflation.\par \par \uc2 In the over-the-counter market, the dollar was at CNY6.4390 around 0830 GMT, up from Wednesday's close of CNY6.4341. It traded between CNY6.4344 and CNY6.4491.\par \par \uc2 "The yuan's appreciation trend won't change as inflation pressure is still weighing," said a Beijing-based trader with a local bank.\par \par \uc2 China is set to release inflation data for July on Aug. 9. The consumer price index is widely expected to rise at around the same pace as June's 6.4% increase, its fastest in three years.\par \par \uc2 The yuan appreciated about 2.3% against the dollar in the first half of the year, which helped offset higher global commodity prices and imported inflation pressures, the Financial News, a central-backed paper, said in a commentary Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Even though the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen's appreciation, "I don't think more central banks will follow suit as many are facing imported inflation pressure as China is," a Shanghai-based foreign bank trader said.\par \par \uc2 After Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, the dollar quickly rose against the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 0836 GMT, the ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 74.688, up from 74.032.\par \par \uc2 Offshore, one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forwards rose to 6.3680/6.3730 from 6.3580/6.3620 late Wednesday, implying a 1.1% rise by the yuan against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next year.\par \par \uc2 In the offshore yuan market in Hong Kong, where the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely, the dollar-yuan exchange rate was at 6.4228 late Thursday, down from 6.4265 late Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 -Wynne Wang and Rose Yu contributed to this article, Dow Jones Newswires; (86-21) 6120-1200; wynne.wang@dowjones.com [ 04-08-11 0929GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1153   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110804e784000xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
