\par\par \b \uc2 UK gilts slip after BoJ  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 347 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 10:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 4 (Reuters) - British gilt futures slipped on Thursday, with yields on 10-year gilts backing away from a record-low after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the rise of the yen fuelled hopes of coordinated policy action to ease pressure on European debt markets.\par \par \uc2 Japan sold 1 trillion yen ($12.6 billion) and its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased monetary policy on Thursday, joining Switzerland in efforts to tame \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  buoyed by safe-haven demand from investors fretting about the health of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "There's nothing UK-specific, it's a broader move in the market. It's partly due to the actions of the SNB and BoJ, which is putting a bit of a backstop on some of the imbalances that were creeping into the market, and suggesting the possibility that the policy authorities in general might be set to become more active from here," said Nomura strategist Sean Maloney.\par \par \uc2 At 0811 GMT, the September gilt future was 25 ticks down at 126.04, lagging the equivalent Bund by a few ticks.\par \par \uc2 In the cash market, the yield on 10-year gilts was up more than 3 basis points at 2.773 percent, backing away from a record low of 2.707 percent struck on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Central banks will remain in the spotlight this session, with the Bank of England concluding a two-day monthly meeting at 1100 GMT. Most economists expect no change to policy, but there is an outside chance they will discuss a second wave of quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 --------------------- KEY MARKET DATA--------------------------- L