\par\par \b \uc2 HIGHLIGHTS-BOJ eases monetary policy, supports  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 07:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 4 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased monetary policy by boosting its asset buying scheme on Thursday at a rate review that was cut short by one day in the wake of Tokyo's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 The nine-member board made the decision by a unanimous vote.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintained its benchmark policy rate at a range of zero to 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Following are highlights of the BOJ's decision to buy more assets:\par \par \uc2 -- The BOJ increased the size of its asset buying programme introduced in October last year to 15 trillion yen ($195 billion) from 10 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 -- This is the BOJ's direct, short-term monetary policy tool with which it tries to push down one- to two-year bond yields and risk premiums by purchasing government bonds and private debt such as corporate bonds, commercial paper and trust funds investing in property and stocks.\par \par \uc2 -- It also increased the size of a cheap loan programme offering fixed-rate funds via its market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in place since December 2009, to 35 trillion yen from 30 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 -- The size of the fund that combines asset buying and market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  backed by collateral was, as a result of these two moves, raised to 50 trillion yen from 40 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 -- The BOJ plans to complete the purchases by the end of next year, instead of June next year as previously pledged.\par \par \uc2 -- In the asset-buying programme, the BOJ increased purchase targets to the following (previous figures in parentheses):\par \par \uc2 Government bonds and bills 8.5 trillion yen (5 trillion yen)\par \par \uc2 Commercial paper and corporate bonds 5 trillion yen (4 trillion yen)\par \par \uc2 Exchange-traded funds (ETF) 1.4 trillion yen (900 billion yen)\par \par \uc2 Real estate investment trusts (REIT) 110 billion yen (100 billion yen)\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 76.890 Japanese Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Leika Kihara and Rie Ishiguro; Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (HIGHLIGHTS)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110807e78400043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
