\par\par \b \uc2 JGBs pare gains after BOJ easing, economy worries support\b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 04:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 4 (Reuters) - Japanese government bonds rose for the third straight day on Thursday, pushing the 10-year yield below one percent on signs of trouble in the global economy, though they trimmed gains after easing steps by the Bank of Japan disappointed some players.\par \par \uc2 Maturities over 10 years continued to outperform sharply as investors who held off buying on economic recovery hopes rushed to buy the sector, doubting that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets will have a long lasting effect in stemming the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, cutting its two-day meeting short to end it on Thursday, said it would increase its asset purchase by five trillion yen to 15 trillion yen, in line with market expectations.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, did not expand the target of its bond buying in the asset purchase programme as some had hoped, leading to disappointment in the market.\par \par \uc2 Ten-year JGB futures trimmed gains to trade at 142.31, still up 0.08 point on the day but down from Thursday's peak of 142.49, their highest in nine months.\par \par \uc2 The 10-year JGB yield rose to 1.015 percent from a nine-month low of 0.995 percent hit earlier in the day and up 0.5 basis point on the day.\par \par \uc2 The 20-year yield stood at 1.745 percent , down 1.0 basis point on the day.\par \par \uc2 Worries over anaemic growth in the United States and the euro zone's debt crisis are likely to continue to support the bond market and market players say the Bank of Japan's steps will have limited impact on allaying those fears.\par \par \uc2 "It remains to be seen whether the easing will actually temper the yen's strength. The market focus has already shifted to what the BOJ may have to do if yen strength persists," said Koichi Ono, senior strategist at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Hideyuki Sano; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-JGB (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110807e7840001h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
