\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-BOJ expected to ease monetary policy -source\b0\par\par\uc2 340 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 03:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Most likely step is to top up asset buying scheme\par \par \uc2 * BOJ cuts short rate review, announcement on Thursday\par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 4 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan is expected to ease monetary policy at a rate review on Thursday that was cut short to one day after Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to weaken the yen, a source familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking said.\par \par \uc2 If the BOJ were to ease policy, the most likely step would be to top up its 10 trillion yen ($130 billion) asset buying scheme, which it defines as the benchmark for monetary easing, by 5 trillion.\par \par \uc2 There is a possibility it may boost the scheme to double that amount, although this would be tough as the BOJ already buys a sizable chunk in relatively small markets for risk assets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  solo in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem what it saw as one-sided rises in the yen, the finance minister said on Thursday, pushing the dollar sharply up against the yen.\par \par \uc2 "We hope the finance ministry's step helps stabilise financial markets," the BOJ said in a statement issued shortly after the announcement.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the BOJ may aim to surprise markets with additional or bolder steps, such as cutting interest rates to absolute zero from the current range of zero to 0.1 percent, although the possibility of this is small.\par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa has signalled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's readiness to ease policy this week, saying on Wednesday that the bank would take appropriate action by scrutinising the negative impact that recent yen gains could have on the economy.\par \par \uc2 The comment likely reflects growing concerns within the BOJ that the global slowdown and persistent yen rises were already hurting business sentiment and threatening its forecast that Japan's economy will resume a moderate recovery in the autumn.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 76.890 Japanese Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Edmund Klamann)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110807e7840000u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
