\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Plunge; DJIA Sheds More Than 500 Points\b0\par\par\uc2 2,848 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2011\par 00:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11383.68  -512.76  -4.31% \par Nasdaq     2556.39  -136.68  -5.08% \par S&P 500    1200.07   -60.27  -4.78% \par FTSE 100   5393.14  -191.37  -3.43% \par Xetra DAX  6414.76  -225.83  -3.40% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 7/32    +4/32 0.268%  -6.4BP \par 5Y Tsy 101 30/32   +22/32 1.100% -14.1BP \par 10Y Tsy 106 2/32 +1 18/32 2.423% -17.4BP \par  \par USD/JPY    78.94-98  +2.47% \par Range      80.24-76.98 \par EUR/USD    1.4104-06  -1.52% \par Range      1.4370-1.4094 \par AUD/USD    1.0473-78  -2.61% \par Range      1.0778-1.0458 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.80-11.30% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    13.80-14.65% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    13.60-14.80% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,659.00    -$7.30 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,642.20-$1,684.90 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $86.63    -$5.30 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks plunged, driving the Dow Jones Industrial Average to close down more than 500 points, as investors appeared to lose faith in the ability of the world's policy makers to revive the global economy and stave off a rolling debt crisis in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow finished just off session lows with a 512.76-point decline, or 4.31%, to 11383.68, erasing all its gains for 2011. The slump of the past few weeks has driven the Dow down more than 11% from its April closing highs--putting the index officially in correction territory.\par \par \uc2 It was the measure's biggest single-day point loss since Dec. 1, 2008, when the Dow plunged 679.95 points at the height of the financial crisis, one of the market's worst days ever.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index fell 60.27 points, or 4.78%, to 1200.07, the eighth loss in nine sessions. The S&P and Nasdaq Composite also moved into correction territory. The Nasdaq slumped 136.68 points, or 5.08%, to 2556.39, also in the red for the year.\par \par \uc2 The Chicago Board Options Exchange Volatility Index, known as the "fear gauge," broke the 30 level for the first time since March 16, rising 35% to 31.66.\par \par \uc2 Investors across the globe have been buffeted by economic and political turmoil in recent days. In the U.S., fears have turned from worries about a possible default by the U.S. government to a weakening economic outlook. A string of data have pointed to a slowing of the recovery, and investors are now bracing for the closely watched non-farm payroll report on Friday.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, leaders are struggling to contain a growing debt crisis. Investors are increasingly worried that troubles are spreading to Italy and Spain, driving down stocks across the region and sending borrowing costs of peripheral nations soaring.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., all of the Dow stocks were lower as investors sold across the board. All the S&P 500 sectors were in the red, with only three of the 500 components ending the day higher. Stocks kept cascading to fresh lows throughout the session.\par \par \uc2 "This is a fear-driven market," said Christian Thwaites, president and chief executive at Sentinel Investments. "It's not a Black Monday, or Black Thursday, but it's in pretty bad shape--all the big stocks are being liquidated."\par \par \uc2 On the floor of the New York Stock Exchange, the mood was somber and tense. "There is no reason to buy U.S. cash equities," said Doreen Mogavero, president of Mogavero Lee & Co. "[Investors have] come to the point where we find out we could be looking at ten more years of austerity."\par \par \uc2 Underscoring that worried investors are increasingly seeking cold cash, the \uc2 Bank of New York\uc2  is preparing to charge some large depositors to hold their funds.\par \par \uc2 The bank cited the massive dollar deposits it has received over recent weeks, as investors and corporations retreat from financial markets amid Europe's debt crisis and the recent debate over U.S. government borrowing.\par \par \uc2 Kraft Foods Inc\uc2 . spent much of the session as the blue-chip Dow's only gainer but ultimately fell 1.5%. Kraft said it would split into two publicly traded companies.\par \par \uc2 General Motors Co\uc2 . shed 4.3% as executives warned of a weaker second half and a slowing U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Asian markets are expected to be hit after the falls on overseas markets, Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson said Friday.\par \par \uc2 "The main theme seems to be the global economy may relapse into a recession, that's really driven investors to sell," Williamson said, adding U.S. non-farm payrolls data, to be released later in the global trading day, "is critical as to whether it brings some stability back into equity markets."\par \par \uc2 Asian shares were mixed Thursday. China's shares ended higher as investors bought issues battered by recent sell-offs, but gains were limited by persistent concerns that upcoming macroeconomic data will show a slowing domestic economy and high inflation.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares fell for a third straight session because of continued concerns about the outlook for global economic growth.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks ended mixed Thursday as the buoyant effect of a long-awaited \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese government was offset by worries over a U.S. economic slowdown and the ongoing European debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 "Ultimately, fundamentals dictate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates, and for now that means the state of the U.S. economy," says Mitsuhige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management, noting that the market is also keenly tuned into any possible ratings downgrade on U.S. Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  and \uc2 Kirin Holdings\uc2  are among the companies reporting results Friday.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures prices shed more than 5% in U.S. trading Thursday, plunging to their lowest level since February in a broad market sell-off spurred by intensifying fears that the world's major economies are stalling.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices fluctuated by more than $40 in U.S. trade Thursday, reaching new highs in the morning before dropping back to nearly $1,640 a troy ounce, as a selloff across commodities and stocks had investors cashing out of precious metals.\par \par \uc2 Copper slumped to its lowest close in five weeks on the London Metal Exchange Thursday as bearish comments by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet exacerbated persistent concerns over economic growth in the region, sparking a broad-based market slide and sending the euro firmly into the red.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A decision by the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to restart buying of troubled euro-zone bonds did little to soothe investors or the euro, which hit a new record low against the Swiss franc as markets feared that Europe's long-festering debt drama could be taking a turn for the worse.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets were initially captivated by Japan's surprise yen-selling foray, which came without the support of other major economies. However, frenzied selling of high-yielding assets amid global sovereign-debt woes yanked support from under the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and triggered broad safe-haven buying of the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the global growth outlook darkening, the ECB held interest rates unchanged, as most analysts expected. But its semi-dovish language--including an acknowledgment that uncertainty had heightened considerably--unnerved investors who interpreted it as a policy shift.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone's comparatively higher interest rates have been the sole incentive for investors to hold the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --particularly as fears grow that the problems that struck Greece, Ireland and Portugal could soon spread to Spain and Italy. As a result, the perception that the ECB could be less aggressive in tightening monetary policy hit the euro hard.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Furthermore, while the ECB did purchase the debt of financially strapped euro-zone countries, some questioned why it did nothing to support the bonds of Italy and Spain, two of the euro zone's largest members and currently in the crosshairs of jittery sellers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Both what [the ECB] did and didn't do was interpreted badly," said Alessio de Longis, vice president and portfolio manager at Oppenheimer Funds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's action Thursday "was a euro negative," he added. With the global economy heading for a slump and inflation seemingly declining, "there's less of a case for tightening monetary policy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the Dow plunging by more than 500 points in aggressive late-day selling, the beleaguered greenback reverted to its form as a safe haven, albeit of last resort. Both the Swiss franc and the yen have become the premier safety assets, despite efforts from Switzerland and Japan to undercut the strength of their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday in New York, the euro was at $1.4100 from $1.4322 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y78.90 from Y77.07, while the euro was at Y111.27 from Y110.37. The U.K. pound was at $1.6260 from $1.6424. The dollar was at CHF0.7628 from CHF0.7705. The euro plunged to a new record low in late trading at CHF1.0769.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 75.252 from 74.044.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unexpected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was forced to take a backseat to the anxiety that drove major stock benchmarks deep into correction territory.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The world's third-largest economy sold its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to prevent it from undercutting a fragile economic recovery. The move drew stern rebukes from both the U.S. and Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, in an environment of acute risk aversion, analysts doubted Japan's efforts would have a lasting impact. With the dollar having lost some of its safe-haven premium, investors have turned more frequently to the yen. That dynamic was likely to counteract any efforts to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --especially if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  without the support of other Group of Seven economies.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is the worst possible timing to try and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market when you have a safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which has also been under pressure," said senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Michael Woolfolk at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of New York\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It's widely known that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rarely has long-term impact and in these market conditions it's unlikely to have even short-term impact."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A gloomy global economic outlook sent investors on a mass exodus out of risky markets Thursday and into the refuge of U.S. government debt, sending yields to yet another round of new 2011 lows. [ 04-08-11 2212GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The intense flight into safe-haven Treasurys forced benchmark 10-year yields down more than 50 basis points since last Friday; there hasn't been such a large one-week drop in yields since November and December of 2008, when \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lehman Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2  collapsed, sparking a financial-market frenzy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These days, it's the ongoing euro-zone credit crisis and mounting fears about a global economic slowdown fueling the scramble into safety. Euro-zone officials can't find a viable solution to contain their tenuous debt situation, while the newly passed U.S. budget plan is heightening worries that there will be less fiscal stimulus, denting an already-struggling U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These anxieties have driven longer-dated Treasurys prices up for five straight sessions, culminating in Thursday's rally that coincided with tanking stocks, plunging oil prices and a highly turbulent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's a big trade happening globally--one that's characterized by fear," said Richard Bryant, head of U.S. Treasury trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global\f28 \uc2 \f2 , adding that Thursday's Treasurys rally involves large-scale buying across investors and investment accounts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bryant said Treasury yields are so low--with 2-year rates at a new all-time low--that investors are looking to longer maturities for returns. "We're running out of room," he said. He sees 10-year yields going as low as 2.40%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Michael Mata, senior portfolio manager of the ING \f28 \uc2 \f2 Global Bond Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 , with more than $500 million under management, said his team lowered its 10-year yield forecast to 2.60% last Thursday. Since then, yields have fallen swiftly below that mark.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're pricing in a recession within the developed economies," Mata said, adding that if Friday's non-farm payrolls report falls short of expectations, markets will begin expecting the Federal Reserve to make a move.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With prices as high as they are, the set of U.S. debt auctions next week will be a major test of investor appetite. The Treasury Department is due to sell $72 billion in 3-, 10- and 30-year notes and bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Thursday afternoon trading in New York, benchmark 10-year notes rose 1 18/32 in price, as yields sank 17 basis points to 2.423%. The 30-year bond advanced 3 15/32 in price, with the yield falling 18 basis points to 3.690%. Both yields set new 2011 lows, with levels not seen since last October. Two-year notes were up 4/32 to yield 0.268%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond prices fell in Tokyo Thursday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen and the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut short a policy board meeting to announce further easing measures that failed to impress investors expecting more aggressive steps.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government launched a yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in morning trade in Tokyo, sending the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down sharply against its counterparts. The BOJ followed the MOF by increasing its six-month fixed rate purchases by Y5 trillion and asset-purchase fund by Y5 trillion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Any further moves by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the months ahead "depend on how global financial markets and the Japanese economy develop," said Naomi Hasegawa, senior fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "If the global financial market falls into another abyss or the Japanese economy runs out of steam, there will be pressure for the BOJ to do more."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Thursday after the PBOC injected net liquidity totaling CNY44 billion into the banking system for the third straight week via the regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to ease investors' concerns about liquidity, a Shenzhen-based trader with a local broker said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead, investors will focus next week on July inflation data, due Tuesday, to see if inflationary pressure peaked in the first half, the trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Friday, Indonesia's economy likely grew 6.47% on the year in the second quarter, slightly slower than the 6.5% growth posted in the first quarter, a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 12 economists shows. The economy likely expanded 2.82% on the quarter, faster than the 1.5% growth posted in the first quarter, according to a forecast of 10 economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's consumer price index likely rose 1.93% on the year in July, unchanged from June's rise, according to a Dow Jones poll of 12 economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Bank Of NY Charging Big Depositors To Hold Cash \par US Jobless Claims Barely Changed At 400,000 \par BoE Keeps Rates On Hold As Outlook Darkens \par Japan Acts To Weaken Yen, BoJ Eases Further \par AIG Posts $1.84 Billion 2Q Profit, But Misses Estimates \par Ally Financial IPO Delayed Until At Least Sep \par Bank Charge Sends T-bill Yields Back To Zero \par Paulson Fund Down 15% YTD Through July \par GM Posts Large Profit On Strong Global Sales \par Report Italian Prosecutors Seize Rating Firms' Documents \par Kraft Foods To Split Into 2 Companies \par Apple Becomes Top Global Smartphone Maker \par Fed Balance Sheet Broadly Steady In Latest Week \par China Everbright Bank Given Waiver On 25% Public Float By Bourse For HK IPO - Source \par IMF: South Korean Economic Outlook Remains 'Favorable' \par Nissan To Merge 2 Domestic Dealership Networks -Nikkei \par JX Allying With South Korea's Top Oil Refiner -Nikkei \par Japan Puts New Nuclear Export Talks On Hold -Nikkei \par Steelmakers To Lift 2nd-Half Output As Auto Production Rallies -Nikkei \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2330  Australia    Jul Australian PCI \par 2350  Japan        Jul Provisional Trade Statistics for 1st 20 days of \par 2350  Japan        Jul International Reserves / Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par 0100  Philippines  Jul CPI \par 0130  Australia    Reserve Bank of Australia Quarterly Statement on Monetary Policy \par 0200  Indonesia    2Q  GDP \par 0500  Japan        Jun Indexes of Business Conditions - Preliminary \par                    Release \par 0645  France       Jun Foreign trade \par 0730  Thailand     Weekly International Reserves \par 0800  Taiwan       Jul WPI \par 0800  Taiwan       Jul CPI \par 0820  Taiwan       Jul International Reserves \par 0830  UK           Jul UK producer prices \par 0830  UK           2Q  Insolvency statistics for the second quarter of 2011 \par 0900  Malaysia     Jul International Reserves, end of month \par 1000  Germany      Jun Industrial Production Index \par 1130  India        Weekly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves \par 1230  US           Jul Unemployment rate \par 1900  US           Jun Consumer Credit \par N/A   Philippines  Jul Gross International Reserves \par N/A   Philippines  2Q  Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas - Bangko Sentral ng \par                    Pilipinas Inflation Report \par N/A   UK           Jul Halifax House Price Index \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 59