\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 20:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par STOCKS, OIL PLUNGE AMID GLOOMY ECONOMIC OUTLOOK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commodities, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and equities markets are in a state of near-panic as central banks take increasingly drastic steps to salvage the global economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK OF NY CHARGING BIG DEPOSITORS TO HOLD CASH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of New York\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the biggest U.S. custodial bank, says it will begin slapping a fee next week on customers who have vastly increased their deposit balances over the past month, citing massive dollar deposits it's received over recent weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB REACTIVATES ANTI-CRISIS MEASURES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet announces that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will offer a six-month tender of unlimited size next week, while traders report a re-opening of its program of government bond buying.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US JOBLESS CLAIMS BARELY CHANGED AT 400,000 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number of people claiming new jobless benefits was little changed from the week before, pointing to persistent weakness in the U.S. labor market. Claims total 400,000, down from a revised 401,000 the week before; economists expected claims to rise to 405,000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOE KEEPS RATES ON HOLD AS OUTLOOK DARKENS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England keeps its key interest rate at a record low for the 30th straight month against a backdrop of slackening growth in the U.K. and gloomier prospects for the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN ACTS TO WEAKEN YEN, BOJ EASES FURTHER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's government launches a yen-selling campaign backed up by Y10 trillion in easing measures from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as officials say they need to tame market speculation threatening a fragile economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GM POSTS LARGE PROFIT ON STRONG GLOBAL SALES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Auto maker says its 2Q profit nearly doubles to $2.5 billion, or $1.54 a share, as revenue soars, bolstered by stronger global sales and an increase in production of high-margin pickup trucks and SUVs in North America. Analysts forecast $1.20 a share. Shares drop 2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KRAFT FOODS\f28 \uc2 \f2  TO SPLIT INTO 2 COMPANIES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kraft is splitting in two, putting its global snacks and North American grocery businesses in separate baskets. The surprise move is CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Irene Rosenfeld\f28 \uc2 \f2 's second bold step in as many years to transform the company. Shares up 2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NISSAN TO MERGE 2 DOMESTIC DEALERSHIP NETWORKS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  will combine its two domestic sales channels in a few years, consolidating all 2,200 Japanese dealerships under a single Nissan brand, The Nikkei reports.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RIO TINTO POSTS RECORD 1H NET, RAISES BUYBACK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 1H earnings surge to $7.6 billion even as it warns of sovereign-debt risk in the U.S. and Europe. The world's second-largest iron ore miner also lifts its share buyback plan to $7 billion from $5 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par Going Public \par US INVESTORS AREN'T KEEN ON THIS WEEK'S IPOS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors aren't loving the pair of IPOs that were scheduled for this week. Online benefit software firm WageWorks scales back its price range and concierge health care provider WhiteGlove Health Inc. once again pushes off its offering by a week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Al's Emporium \par MY ECONOMICS GURU GOT AHEAD OF HIMSELF \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economist Robert Aliber in January predicted the U.S. economy would grow 4.5% to 5% in 2011, the stock market would rise 15% and unemployment would fall to 7%. Now he concedes his rosy outlook was 'excessively optimistic,' Al Lewis writes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Heard on the Street \par SWISS RE'S POST-EARTHQUAKE BLOOM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earthquakes have rattled reinsurers this year; shares in the world's big players are down 10-15%. But \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss Re\f28 \uc2 \f2  offers on the market's three biggest fears: huge natural catastrophe losses, weak pricing and spare industry capacity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interview \par MOODY'S SAYS JAPAN ACTION 'POSITIVE', CHALLENGES REMAIN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Easing measures by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen are "positive actions," but their impact is likely limited and they don't remove the "big credit challenges" the country faces, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par   ============ MARKETS ACTION ============ \par  \par US STOCKS PLUNGE, DOW FALLS OVER 300 POINTS AS FEARS GRIP MARKET \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares plunge as global economic fears grip market. DJIA slumps 3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INVESTORS HIDE OUT IN US TREASURYS, PUSHING YIELDS TO NEW 2011 LOWS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys rally, as yields fall to new 2011 lows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB'S TRICHET UNDERWHELMS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKET AS BOND-BUYING REVIVED \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro declines against the dollar and Swiss franc after ECB leaves rates unchanged.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OIL  PLUNGES BELOW $89/BBL, AT LOWEST SINCE FEB \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures sink to less than $89 as dollar surges amid US economic jitters.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPOT GOLD FALTERS AFTER NEW RECORD HIGH AT $1,682.14/OZ \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spot gold slumps amid market-wide losses after hitting all-time high. [ 04-08-11 1800GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110804e784001ij\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
