\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares End Mostly Down;Nikkei Stock Average Up On Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 10:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Sarah Turner in Sydney and V. Phani Kumar in Hong Kong\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (MarketWatch) - South Korean and Australian stocks were among Asian markets ending sharply lower Thursday on fears about the U.S. economic outlook, but a key Japanese benchmark edged up after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Stock Average overcame a lackluster start and jumped more than 1% during the session, as the yen slumped against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its recent rise.\par \par \uc2 But the benchmark finished the day just 0.2% higher at 9,659.18, amid skepticism the measures can keep the yen down for long and as the size of additional monetary easing announced by the Bank of Japan fell short of some expectations.\par \par \uc2 "The extent of the easing measures decided today was somewhat disappointing," said Junko Nishioka, chief economist at RBS Securities Japan, referring to the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to pour another Y10 trillion into the economy, by increasing its asset purchase fund by Y5 trillion and adding another Y5 trillion in fixed-rate six-month loans, which currently total Y10 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Nishioka said that the day's actions weren't enough to ease the upward pressure on the yen - a trend which hurts Japan's exporters - because of factors including weakness in the U.S. economy and European fiscal debt issues.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed action by the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  to trim its three-month Libor rate target close to zero in an effort to stem the Swiss franc's strength.\par \par \uc2 Several major Japanese exporters got a lift from the news, with \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . rising 0.6%, \uc2 Nikon Corp\uc2 . adding 1.2% and \uc2 Nintendo Co\uc2 . shares gaining 1.1%.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the Asian region, South Korea's Kospi tumbled 2.3% to 2,018.47 for a third straight day of losses exceeding 2%. The day's performance gave the index a net loss of 1.6% in 2011 to date.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX shed 1.3% to 4,276.5, Taiwan's Taiex shed 1.7% to 8,317.27 and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index dropped 0.5% to end at 21,884.74.\par \par \uc2 China's Shanghai Composite Index ended 0.2% higher at 2,684.04.\par \par \uc2 Many regional stocks gave up their early gains to finish lower as investors looked toward Friday's July non-farm payrolls data in the U.S. with a sense of caution.\par \par \uc2 "Barring a significant upside surprise in the U.S., nonfarm payrolls data due on Friday, we do not expect any change in this weak momentum," RBS economists wrote in a note.\par \par \uc2 Outside of Japan, several exporters remained under selling pressure, with \uc2 Hyundai Motor Co\uc2 . losing 2.3%, and \uc2 Kia Motors Corp\uc2 . shrinking 3%, while \uc2 Foxconn Technology Co\uc2 . and \uc2 Asustek Computer Inc\uc2 . tumbled 3.7% each in Taipei.\par \par \uc2 Several financial shares were among the decliners, with \uc2 Australia & New Zealand Banking Group Ltd\uc2 . falling 2.4% and \uc2 National Australia Bank Ltd\uc2 . shedding 2.3% in Sydney, while \uc2 HSBC Holdings PLC\uc2  dropped 1.0% and Bank of Communications Co. shed 0.9% in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Chinatrust Financial Holding Co\uc2 . lost 1% in Taipei, and Woori Finance Holdings Co. declined 1.8% in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 Some resource-sector stocks moved in line with oil and copper prices, which gave ground in regular trading in New York, as worries about economic growth sparked concern about future commodity demand.\par \par \uc2 Japanese oil major \uc2 Inpex Corp\uc2 . declined 2.1% despite the firm posting strong quarterly earnings and raising its profit outlook Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Woodside Petroleum Ltd\uc2 . shed 1.9% in Sydney, while \uc2 Cnooc Ltd\uc2 . dropped 2.4% in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 -Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; markettalk@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 04-08-11 0843GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1106 4125 5037 62560   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110804e784000ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
