\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 917 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 09:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ TAKES ADDITIONAL EASING ACTION; ADDS Y10T IN SPECIAL FUNDING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan decided to expand its special fund for asset purchases and liquidity supplying \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by a total of Y10 trillion at the end of its shortened policy board meeting Thursday in a response to increasing concerns over the negative impact of the strong yen on the economy. (published 0520 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ EXPANDS ASSET-BUYING AND LOANS IN FACE OF STRONG YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan decided to expand its special fund for asset purchases and low-cost loans by a total of Y10 trillion Thursday to mitigate the negative impact of the strong yen on the economy. (published 2347 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par WSJ: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOVES TO WEAKEN THE YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday morning in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stem the rise of the yen against the dollar, hoping to preserve modest growth in an economy rebounding surprisingly well from the March earthquake and tsunami--but that now appears threatened by a soaring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  undermining exporters. (published 0153 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: IMPROVING NEW ZEALAND 2Q EMPLOYMENT DATA ADD TO SUPPORT FOR ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's unemployment rate was steady in the second quarter, providing some support for the economic recovery despite a devastating earthquake that struck the nation's second largest city of Christchurch in February. (published 2323 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UPDATE: CHINA PENSION FUND TO INVEST CNY8 BLN IN CORPORATE BONDS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's national pension fund plans to invest CNY8 billion in domestic corporate bonds and targets a cumulative yield of 30% over five years, state-run Shanghai Securities News reported Thursday. (published 0359 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ECB EXPECTED TO SIGNAL GOVT BOND BUYING INTENTION - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to signal it is stepping into the euro zone debt crisis on Thursday by reopening its purchases of government debt, amid fears the turmoil will claim the economy of a nation that is "too big to bail," the U.K.'s Daily Telegraph reported Thursday. (published 0053 GMT)\\\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA FLAGS EXTERNAL RISKS, PLEDGES TO CONTAIN INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's Ministry of Strategy and Finance Thursday reiterated that it will closely monitor economic conditions and ensure price stability. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA TO KEEP BOOSTING YUAN FLEXIBILITY TO FIGHT INFLATION - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China will use various monetary policy tools to combat inflation expectations, including further boosting the flexibility of the yuan exchange rate, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -backed paper said in a commentary Thursday. (published 0106 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER: CHINA CAN TOLERATE HIGHER INFLATION - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should be able to tolerate slightly higher levels of inflation as its economy shows strong growth, a state researcher said in a newspaper commentary Thursday. (published 0257 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INVESTOR JIM ROGERS DOWN ON US DEBT DEAL, UP ON A$ \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Commodities and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  investor Jim Rogers said Thursday that the debt ceiling agreement reached in the U.S. will come back to haunt that country's economy but optimistic about the prospects for Australian and its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (published 0024 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INTERVIEW: HIGH KIWI A HEADWIND FOR ECONOMIC RECOVERY - NZ FINANCE MINISTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The high New Zealand dollar is a headwind for the economy, New Zealand Finance Minister Bill English reiterated Thursday, but said there was little the government could do to limit its gains. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: MALAYSIA JUNE EXPORTS +8.6% ON YEAR; +6.0% EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's June exports grew 8.6% from a year earlier as a sharp increase in palm oil, petroleum and gas exports offset a decline in electronic shipments. (published 0348 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par SINGAPORE MINISTER: GLOBAL ECONOMIC ENVIRONMENT HAS BECOME DICHOTOMOUS, VOLATILE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The global economic environment has become dichotomous and volatile with many developed countries still on a slow road to recovery while Asia continues to log strong growth, S. Iswaran, Singapore's minister in the prime minister's office, said Thursday. (published 0254 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  REITERATES NO PLAN TO USE EXCHANGE RATE TO CURB INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Thailand\f28 \uc2 \f2  reiterated Wednesday it didn't plan to use exchange rates as a tool to combat escalating price pressures. (published 0308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  GOVERNOR: TO TRY TO LOWER LOAN RATES FROM SEP -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 State Bank of Vietnam\f28 \uc2 \f2  will work with local credit institutions to find ways to lower the interest rates on their loans from next month, local media said Thursday. (published 0406 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par VIETNAM NEEDS $48.8 BLN FOR POWER PROJECTS IN 2011-2020 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vietnam will need to invest $48.8 billion in its power industry between this year and 2020, the Ministry of Industry and Trade said Thursday. (published 0234 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par NEW FOCUS AUTO TECH TO SELL AROUND CNY200 MLN OFFSHORE YUAN BONDS - SOURCE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Focus Auto Tech Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . plans to raise around CNY200 million by selling offshore yuan-denominated bonds with maturities of two years, a person familiar with the situation said Thursday. (published 0336 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: INDONESIA 2Q GDP LIKELY SLOWED SLIGHTLY AS PRIVATE CONSUMPTION EASED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's economic growth likely eased slightly in the second quarter because private consumption, particularly of cars and motorcycles, slowed following the natural disasters that hit Japan earlier this year. (published 0429 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: TAIWAN JULY INFLATION SEEN MODERATE; EXPORTS LIKELY DOWN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's inflation pressure likely remained moderate in July as food prices fell after rising sharply in June, while exports growth likely slowed due to softening demand for the island's electronics and petrochemical products. (published 0242 GMT) [ 04-08-11 0702GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110804e784000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\