\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Yen Plummets As Japan MOF Steps Into Market\b0\par\par\uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 07:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("WORLD \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Yen Plummets As Japan MOF Steps Into Market, at 0352 GMT misstated the value of the dollar against the Swiss franc in the 10th and 11th paragraphs. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Martin Vaughan \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--After days of fretting publicly over the strength of the yen, Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Thursday to let some air out of the lofty \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't unexpected, but the timing took some by surprise as it came at the outset of what was to be a two-day policy meeting by the Bank of Japan. It also came as the yen was holding steady against its rivals, even though Japanese officials had said repeatedly they were looking for signs of yen volatility or sharp, one-sided movements.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar and euro jumped sharply higher against the yen, with the greenback at Y79.03 lately versus Y77.13 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action. The euro was at Y113.04 versus Y110.72 just before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dealers said the MOF bought dollars around the Y78.35 level through both Japanese and foreign banks, injecting around Y500 billion into the market.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan abruptly shortened its policy meeting, announcing it would now be limited to a single day and would wrap up Thursday afternoon. That has stoked speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will announce policy-easing measures aimed at further diluting the yen.\par \par \uc2 The task for MOF will get tougher as the dollar moves above Y79.00 and it becomes tempting for exporters to sell dollars and for Japanese retail investors to exit long-dollar positions, \uc2 Westpac Banking Corp\uc2 . chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Robert Rennie said.\par \par \uc2 "What we've seen so far is a skirmish in what could become a war of attrition," he said.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have eyed the strengthening yen with growing alarm in recent weeks as debt problems in the U.S. and euro zone have lessened the appeal of the yen's main rivals and boosted its value, along with that of the safe-haven Swiss franc.\par \par \uc2 In contrast to March's coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by G-7 authorities to weaken the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is acting alone this time, though it did consult with other countries before moving.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also rose against the euro and Swiss franc after Tokyo's dollar-buying. That move is sure to be welcomed by \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  officials, who announced aggressive easing measures Wednesday only to see the dollar return to CHF0.7644 in New York trading, close to where it was before the announcement.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was trading at CHF0.7769 on Thursday, up from CHF0.7702 late in New York.\par \par \uc2 Looming in the background are intensifying worries about the spread of euro zone debt problems as Italy's borrowing costs hit an all-time high Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The euro was lower at $1.4289, from $1.4322 in New York trade.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 22:50 EDT / 0250 GMT \par  \par                          Latest         Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par                                         2150 GMT            High    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Japan            78.78-80       77.04-07  +2.25    78.90    76.98  -2.87 \par EUR/USD Euro            1.4314-16      1.4322-25  -0.06   1.4370   1.4310  +6.90 \par GBP/USD U.K.            1.6393-98      1.6424-29  -0.19   1.6440   1.6396  +5.05 \par USD/CHF Switzerland     0.7745-50      0.7701-04  +0.59   0.7765   0.7686 -17.14 \par USD/CAD Canada          0.9636-40      0.9620-25  +0.16   0.9636   0.9606  -3.11 \par AUD/USD Australia       1.0691-96      1.0754-59  -0.58   1.0778   1.0694  +4.49 \par NZD/USD New Zealand     0.8531-36      0.8632-39  -1.18   0.8666   0.8534  +9.45 \par  \par Euro Rates \par  \par EUR/JPY Japan           112.77-82      110.32-39  +2.21   112.97   110.34  +3.83 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Martin Vaughan, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 6415 4033; martin.vaughan@dowjones.com [ 04-08-11 0525GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1034 1863 28038 4162   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110804e784000bk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
