\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: India Short-End OIS Down;1-Year In 8.20%-8.30% Band\b0\par\par\uc2 1,733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 11:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] India short-end overnight-indexed swaps are slightly down on lower crude oil prices and as the wholesale price index for primary articles in India fell 0.6% to 196.9 in the week ended July 23, says a dealer with a state-run bank. The 1-year OIS is at 8.23%/8.26% vs 8.24%/8.28% at Wednesday's close; the 5-year OIS is at 7.41%/7.44% vs 7.40%/7.43% at last close. "The downside in short-end is limited as the on-year rate of food inflation rose" the dealer adds. He tips the one-year to trade in a 8.20%-8.30% band for the session.(sourav.mishra@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0921 GMT [Dow Jones] The Sensex is down 0.8% at 17,795, led by losses in auto stocks after the finance minister told Parliament that the government may look at differential diesel pricing for luxury cars. "The weakness in autos and banks is likely to continue, and a Sensex close below 17,750 today could trigger further losses of 6%-8% over the next one-two months," says A.K. Prabhakar, senior vice president at Anand Rathi Securities. BSE market breadth is slightly negative with 11 losers for every 10 gainers. \uc2 Mahindra & Mahindra\uc2  (500520.BY) is down 3.5% at INR686.40 and \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) is 1.3% lower at iNR915.80. ITC (500875.BY) is down 2.4% at INR201.60 on profit-taking and NTPC (532555.BY) is 2.6% lower at INR176.65 after the firm said its overseas acquisition plans have been hit by high costs and regulations. Among gainers, \uc2 Reliance Infrastructure\uc2  (500390.BY) is up 2.4% at INR573.90 after a electricity regulator Wednesday allowed the firm to recover accumulated costs from its customers. (sudeep.jain@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0919 GMT [Dow Jones] There was good demand for both Spanish bonds, Newedge says, with the 3-year paper covered 2.14 times and the 4-year 2.4 times. Spanish debt "looked extremely cheap versus AAA-rated debt" and both bonds also offered some concession versus surrounding Spanish paper "leaving the issues very close to record cheap levels." Neweage says, "These factors are likely to have attracted investors looking for some good yield pickup during the summer lull for primary market activity." Spanish 10-year bonds are 21bps tighter versus Germany, yielding 6.056%. (mark.brown@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] CZK is a tad firmer at 24.266-24.286 against the euro from 24.29 late Wednesday. "All eyes are on [Thursday's] rate decisions by the ECB and [the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] CNB," says Roman Fol, a trader at Raiffeisebank. "Most other analysts expect the CNB to leave rates unchanged, our analysts think that they could start tightening which in turn would help the koruna firm," he says. If the Czech base rate remains at a record low of 0.75%, "the koruna will stick to its current range with the maximum level at around 24.450," Fol says. (leos.rousek@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0918 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares end down 0.4% at 4122.086 in moderate volume as foreign investors continue taking profits in most blue chips amid concerns over a slow recovery in the global economy, traders say. They expect the market to consolidate Friday and tip the main index in a 4100-4150 range. "The negative Dow Jones futures index inspire investors to take cash to prepare for the worse," says a trader with Reliance Securities. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR565.22 billion worth of shares. Gas distributor Gas Negara (PGAS.JK) is down 5.9% at IDR3,600 on expectations of weak 1H earnings, coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) is down 1.6% at IDR3,150 and car maker Astra (ASII.JK) is down 0.4% at IDR70,400 on profit-taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI ends up 0.1% at 1546.89, easing off its intraday high of 1552.91, on heavy volume amid declines in major regional stock markets; investor sentiment remains weak on concerns over global economic growth after a slew of weak U.S. economic data. Dealers say thin corporate newsflow and negative external environment are weighing on investor sentiment. "Volume will likely be thin ahead of the weekend," says a local dealer. "Trading will be volatile on Friday," the dealer adds. He tips a 1540-1550 range Friday. Market breadth is positive at 397 gainers and 362 losers with total volume of 1.16 billion shares. Among gainers, \uc2 Petronas Chemicals\uc2  (5183.KU) ends up 1.8% at MYR6.91, CIMB Group (1023.KU) is up 0.4% at MYR8.40 and Tenaga Nasional (5347.KU) is up 0.2% at MYR6.06. \uc2 Eastern & Oriental Bhd\uc2  (3417.KU) is off its intraday high to close flat at MYR1.66 after SP Setia (8664.KU) denies news of a takeover bid. Among losers, Gamuda (5398.KU) is down 1.7% at MYR3.54 and \uc2 YTL Power\uc2  (6742.KU) is down 0.5% at MYR1.97. (jason.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0911 GMT [Dow Jones] SNB and MOF actions may help EUR, says BNY Mellon, which notes that in the aftermath of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last September, it was EUR that subsequently proved the mainstream \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of choice among investors. Reserve managers may have concluded that they should use any periods of short-term USD strength as an opportunity to further diversify reserves. With SNB and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limiting the appeal of CHF and JPY, this may, perversely push managers back toward the EUR almost by default, BNY says. EUR/USD at 1.42394. (jessica.mead@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/PHP is higher, lifted by the USD's strong rebound (especially against the JPY) after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The pair is at 42.345 after settling at 42.23 late Wednesday in Asia. Traders expect the USD/PHP to head towards the 42.50 resistance unless positive news comes out of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting later in the global day; support is pegged at 42.10. "If the ECB decides to buy more euro-zone bonds that could be good for risk appetite," says a local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0906 GMT [Dow Jones] Spanish auction is better than expected, says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2 , as Spain sold EUR3.311 billion in 2014 and 2015-dated bonds, versus the EUR2.5 billion to EUR3.5 billion target range. "In sum the auctions were better than one might have expected after the recent trouble in financial markets," says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  analyst Glenn Marci. "The auctions were certainly not cheap, but at least they showed that investor confidence has not completely eroded," he says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0851 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/IDR is higher, reversing the earlier slide after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop the USD/JPY. The pair is now trading at 8,505 vs 8,480 late yesterday, off the 8,470 intraday low. Dealers expect the pair may grind higher toward 8,510 Friday as market players likely will trim short-dollar positions ahead of the weekend. The market will also wait for the 2Q GDP data due around 0200 GMT tomorrow. A Dow Jones poll of 12 economists tip 6.47% on-year growth vs 6.5% in the first quarter. Despite the expected slight slowdown in growth, it remains a healthy expansion, which will continue to attract inflows into Indonesia. "Capital influx to the Indonesian financial markets, both the stock and government bond markets, is likely to continue and thus raise asset prices," says Bank Danamon's economist Anton Hendranata. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is up on foreign banks' buying, with the pair boosted by continued foreign fund outflows from local equities though exporters' selling slowed its rise, two traders say; it is at 28.952 from 28.938 late Wednesday. They say while suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted the pair to its intraday high at 28.952 near the close of the onshore trading session, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also likely sold the greenback intermittently earlier to keep the pair in a narrow range; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually enters both sides of the trade to smooth the the pair's moves and keep it within its own expected ranges. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still pushed the U.S. dollar up during late trade maybe because it thinks the greenback is likely to keep rising against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," one of the traders says, noting the USD's strength against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He tips the pair in a 28.890-28.980 range Friday. Taiwan shares end down 1.7%, extending Wednesday's 1.5% fall; foreign investors were net sellers of NT$7.1 billion worth of local shares. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 15 is tighter, trading at 118 bps, 117 bps vs Wednesday's close of 121.5 bps, as the Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to tame the recent JPY strength, says a CDS trader at a major bank in Tokyo. The recent gains in the JPY are seen as a threat to the Japanese economy that is recovering from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami. Japan's one of major exporters \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . (7203.TO), for instance, traded at 67 bps vs Wednesday's 69 bps. The Bank of Japan's decision to expand its asset purchase program is also a plus for Japanese firms' credit, the trader adds. Elsewhere, the news that Hitachi (6501.TO) and \uc2 Mitsubishi Heavy Industries\uc2  (7011.TO) have been considering merging several of their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent Hitachi protection lower and \uc2 Mitsubishi Heavy Industries\uc2  higher, the trader says. Hitachi is at 66 bps, 67 bps vs 71 bps on July 8, while \uc2 Mitsubishi Heavy Industries\uc2  is at 61 bps.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 04, 2011 05:22 ET (09:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110804e784002no\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
