\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Kospi Down 1.9%; Next Support Near 2003\b0\par\par\uc2 1,871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 07:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0539 GMT [Dow Jones] The Kospi is back on the slide, down 1.9% at 2027.77 in afternoon trade, with analysts saying tipping near-term support at the 250-day moving average around 2003. Foreigners and local institutions sell a net KRW501 billion and KRW140 billion worth in local shares, respectively, heating up the overall selling pressure. Chemical shares are leading declines in the wake of the recent pull back in global crude prices; \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2  (096770.SE) is down 7.5% at KRW197,000 and S-Oil (010950.SE) is 7.1% lower at KRW143,500, while \uc2 LG Chem\uc2  (051910.SE) is down 6.2% at KRW421,500. Bank plays are mixed; \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) is off 1.5% at KRW13,500 and \uc2 Hana Financial\uc2  (086790.SE) is down 0.6% at KRW38,800, while Shinhan Financial (055550.SE) is up 2.2% at KRW50,100 after the company said Wednesday its 2Q net profit more than doubled on-year to KRW964.8 billion. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0532 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of Japan's decision to increase 6-month fixed rate purchases by Y5 trillion and asset-purchase fund by Y5 trillion was largely in line with market expectations, though some speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could announce a new round of quantitative easing prompted some investors to sell JGBs to lock in profits after the outcome, says Naomi Hasegawa, senior fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Any further moves by the BOJ in the months ahead "depend on how global financial markets and the Japanese economy develop," Hasegawa says. "If the global financial market falls into another abyss or the Japanese economy runs out of steam, there will be pressure for the BOJ to do more." The lead September JGB futures contract is up 0.07 at 142.30, while the benchmark 10-year yield is flat at 1.010% vs 1.005% before the announcement. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0532 GMT [Dow Jones] The NZD/USD is sharply lower after Japanese authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, says \uc2 Western Union\uc2  Business Solutions corporate dealing manager Chris Hunter; "this has seen the U.S dollar gain strength." Dealers say Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars around the Y78.35 level through both Japanese and foreign banks, injecting around Y500 billion into the market. The pair is at 0.8508 vs 0.8626 earlier in the morning. Hunter says the major risk event now is the U.S. nonfarm payrolls data due Friday, which the market will be closely watching for further clues on economic recovery. He puts support at 0.8490 with resistance at 0.8600. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares turn lower midday as foreign investors continue taking profits in most blue chips amid concerns over a slow recovery in global economy, traders say. The main index is down 0.4% at 4119.891 in moderate volume, off an intraday high of 4157.252. Traders expect the main index to find support at 4100. "A selloff in commodity-related stocks on lower crude oil prices is leading declines," says a trader with Valbury Securities. Foreigners are net sellers of IDR226 billion worth of shares. Gas distributor Gas Negara (PGAS.JK) is down 5.9% at IDR3,600 on expectations for weak 1H earnings, coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) is down 1.6% at IDR3,150 and car maker Astra (ASII.JK) is down 0.6% at IDR70,200 on profit-taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0526 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: The Indonesian economy likely grew 6.47% on year in the second quarter, a tad slower than 6.5% growth posted in the first quarter, a Dow Jones Newswires poll of 12 economists shows. The economy likely expanded 2.82% on quarter, faster than the 1.5% growth posted in the first quarter, according to a forecast of 10 economists. "Private consumption likely slowed, but this was offset by export and investment growth," says Mandiri Sekuritas economist Aldian Taloputra. Private consumption, especially for cars and motorcycles, likely slowed after natural disasters hit Japan in March. Meanwhile, exports stood at $18.41 billion in June, exports breaching another record high, while private investment rose 22.1% on-year in the second quarter. The data release is due Friday around 0200 GMT. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0526 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/SGD is trading at 1.2090 after breaking the 1.2100 resistance vs 1.2047 in early Asian trade as risk-sensitive Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  take a hit given the uncertainty surrounding the global economy. Investors are increasingly focusing on "how the developed economies, especially the U.S., perform, so there is some pull back in the market from what we saw in the morning when the Singapore dollar was higher," says a trader with a local bank. She tips resistance at 1.2140 and support 1.2030. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [Dow Jones] The BOJ announced further easing steps, but the details were well in line with what the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been expecting, meaning the BOJ's steps failed to impact the market at least directly and in short-term. "Through the effects that the measures will have on the economy we may see the yen weaken down the road, but there is almost no immediate impact," one dealer says. The pair is at 79.32; dealers say Japan MOF is still in the market.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0508 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, buoyed by gains in U.S. stocks Wednesday amid encouraging corporate earning results on the local front. The benchmark PSEi ends up 0.3% at 4501.53, led by the mining & oil counter, which posted a 2.8% gain. Emmanuel Soller, a trader with Equity World, says better economic prospects for the second half could provide further support for the market in the absence of negative news overseas. He tips resistance at 4560 for Friday, with support at 4480. Among the most active stocks Lepanto Mining (LC.PH) ends up 8.2% at PHP1.32, PLDT (TEL.PH) ends up 0.1% at PHP2,400 and Energy Development (EDC.PH) ends off 0.9% at PHP6.60. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0446 GMT [Dow Jones] The STI is down 0.3% at 3120.69 after a morning of choppy trade as investors remain cautious over the global economic outlook, while Wednesday's slight gains on Wall Street came despite further soft data on the U.S.'s struggling economy. Volume shrinks to 587 million shares worth S$600 million by 0430 GMT and market breadth turns negative with 1.8 decliners per gainer. CIMB notes the STI found support at its 50-day moving average around 3110 Wednesday, "and this level is likely to be defended in the coming days." It says although the pullback from the recent 3227 high has been sharp, "we remain bullish for the short- to medium-term as the key support at 3056 (July's low) is intact. This pullback could be viewed as an opportunity to accumulate, especially for aggressive traders with a stop placed below Wednesday's low of 3111." Noble (N21.SG) is the worst performer on the STI, down 3.1% at S$1.75 in active trade, tracking the 7.0% slump in \uc2 Glencore\uc2 's London shares Wednesday. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0444 GMT [Dow Jones] TA Securities says the Malaysian ringgit's strength likely helped imports while Malaysia's exports received a "surprise" boost in June from higher demand for commodities amid rebuilding efforts in Japan; the comments come after the government said June exports grew 8.6% on-year as a sharp increase in palm oil, petroleum and gas exports offset a decline in electronics shipments. The on-year increase was bigger than the 6.0% rise forecast by 13 economists polled earlier by Dow Jones Newswires. Imports in June grew 6.3% on-year. The median forecast in the poll was for a 1.5% expansion. The country's trade surplus for June was at MYR7.60 billion, narrower than May's MYR8.49 billion surplus. "It was quite a surprise in terms of overall exports," says TA Securities economist Patricia Oh who had predicted a 4.5% expansion. She adds that the strengthening MYR resulted in higher purchasing power that boosted imports; "we expect moderate exports growth for the remainder of the year, but that will depend on the global economic situation particularly in the U.S. and Europe." (jason.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0443 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/JPY is likely to rise further as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF/BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but the pair is likely to top out below 80.00, JPMorgan Chase Bank chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Tohru Sasaki says in a note. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  started when the pair was trading around the 77.10-77.20 level earlier. Like the pair's move after the mid-Sept 2010 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "Dollar/yen could rally about 2%-3% initially, but fall back into a downtrend within the next few days," Sasaki says. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF/BOJ will likely continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least until the BOJ announces its monetary policy decision later today or the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could potentially last until London or New York time, he says. "In any case, we continue to believe \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this time will be a one-day event," he says. BOJ's monetary policy action is likely to have almost no impact on the USD/JPY, given the zero interest rate environment, he says. The USD/JPY is at 79.12.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 4 Aug 2011 01:39 EDT DJ  MARKET TALK: Kospi Down 1.9%; Next Support Near -2-\par \par \uc2 0442 GMT [Dow Jones] The Nikkei Stock Average is up 0.8% at 9717.02, little changed from the midday close of 9724.21 as USD/JPY hovers near the low 79-level. "Unlike the post-(March 11) quake \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the one today wasn't globally coordinated and the dollar remains anemic due to economic concerns, so the effect was inevitably limited," says Naoki Fujiwara, fund manager at Shinkin Asset Management. The timing of the yen action was pre-emptive, he adds, saying that the current USD/JPY level will serve as a technical support if Friday's U.S. jobs data disappoints. Players remain on keen watch for further easing measures, possibly later in the day. Konami (9766.TO) is up 4.4% at Y2,279 after the company reported 1Q earnings more than triple the year-ago level. Hitachi (6501.TO) is up 2.2% at Y473 and \uc2 Mitsubishi Heavy Industries\uc2  (7011.TO) is up 3.7% at Y362, both off of their intraday highs. The pair held off announcing merger details originally scheduled for later Thursday, according to a local media report. (judy.lam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 04, 2011 01:39 ET (05:39 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110804e7840019w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
