\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: 10-Yr JGB Yld Breaks 1.000%; BOJ Mtg To End Early\b0\par\par\uc2 1,883 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 04:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] The 10-year JGB yield breaks 1.000% to hit a near nine-month low at 0.995% after the BOJ announces it will conclude its monetary policy board meeting later Thursday instead of Friday as planned, after the MOF conducts JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BOJ's move suggests it will announce additional easing steps, which would support JGBs, analysts say. September JGB futures are up 0.21 at 142.44. "The concerted action between the MOF and the BoJ should be taken as a clear sign that both the government and the BoJ do not want to break the current economic recovery due to the unacceptable yen appreciation," says Masaaki Kanno, chief economist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  in Tokyo. The BOJ will likely announce an expansion of its asset purchase program to Y50 trillion from Y40 trillion, Kanno adds. "We also expect that, together with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BoJ's current account deposits, or the banks' reserves, will increase to indicate that the BoJ is ready to increase liquidity." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] With growing euro-area contagion threatening stability in core countries \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  says the ECB's biggest tool is its Securities and Markets Programme or SMP for government bond purchases. "In theory growing market dislocations in Italian and Spanish markets, and EU political agreement for emergency action, would allow the ECB to reopen the SMP and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm core market stresses and restore liquidity in secondary markets," it says. However, hurdles to such ECB action are high. BBH says such action risks diluting the urgency for fast political action on the EFSF and peripheral fiscal tightening; the exploding crisis means risk on the ECB's balance sheet is growing; ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may slow the transition to a full fiscal union. It says, ultimately the ECB may be forced into action as market contagion has left the political containment strategy woefully inadequate, "though it's more likely that a re-opening of the ECB's SMP would come in as a complement, rather than a substitute, for more political action." (john.phillips@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares will likely rise, helped by a recovery on Wall Street Wednesday and a firmer IDR, traders say. They expect the main index to trade in a 4120-4160 range after it ended down 1.0% Wednesday at 4136.507. "A recovery in many Asian markets so far should also inspire buying but further profit-taking by foreign funds may limit gains of the main index," says a trader with a local securities firm. Coal producer Bumi (BUMI.JK) may lead gains on bargain-buying and hopes for strong 1H earnings. Meanwhile, car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may gain on hopes for strong August car sales. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold prices are likely to hit a new record high in the near term as macroeconomic concerns in the U.S. and Europe encourage sustained safe-haven buying, a Singapore-based trader says. Spot gold is at $1,663.70 a troy ounce, up $2.60 from its previous settlement but off from Wednesday's record high of $1,673.19/oz. The trader says he expects slow U.S. growth to weigh on the USD in the near-term, bolstering demand for alternative safe-haven assets and dollar-denominated commodities including gold; "overall, the U.S. dollar is going to keep gold and other metals elevated." Dollar-priced commodities become cheaper to investors holding other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the greenback is soft. Analysts also expect gold's uptrend to continue in the medium term, with prices buoyed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying as well as safe-haven investment. (clementine.wallop@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/KRW is at 1,056.70, down from 1,058.10 earlier and 1,060.40 late Wednesday in Seoul as the Kospi rebounds (last flat). The \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market against a strong JPY earlier prompted players to anticipate some USD/KRW gains, but "consistent dollar sales from exporters cap its upside quite firmly," says a local bank trader. He tips next support at 1,054. Meanwhile, the JPY/KRW is sharply lower after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , last quoted at 13.4762, from 13.7447 late Wednesday in Seoul. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact looks quite strong. The yen/won cross will likely stay in the 13.4000s for a while," says another trader at a local bank. (jieun.shin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is lower at 28.9140 vs 28.9380 late Wednesday in thin trade, mostly pressured by a weaker USD/KRW, says a local trader. He says today's trading is less active than in recent sessions, "it seems most people are taking a wait-and-see attitude, probably seeking trading incentives from local stocks later," he says, adding if local stocks extend Wednesday's losses, the pair is likely to rise further. He notes \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the lowest USD/CNY central parity rate on record didn't affect the pair much. He says, after many exporters sold heavily above 28.900 Wednesday, selling from exporters is unlikely be to too strong today. He tips the pair in a 28.850-28.950 band for the session. The Taiex is flat after ending down 1.5% Wednesday; foreign investors were net sellers of NT$17.89 billion worth of local shares. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open higher after stocks on Wall Street Wednesday ended an eight-day losing streak. The Shanghai Composite Index is up 0.6% at 2695.75 and faces strong resistance at 2800 amid continued fears of slowing domestic growth and high inflation, says Zhang Guojiang, an analyst at \uc2 Sinolink Securities\uc2  Wealth Management; "uncertainty in the U.S. and Europe have contributed to market weakness in the last few sessions, but valuations in China are low now and investors are less keen to sell." Gold companies are up after gold futures continued their march into record territory for a second day in U.S. trade Wednesday. Zijin Mining (601899.SH) is 1.2% higher at CNY5.70, Zhongjin Gold (600489.SH) gains 1.7% to CNY29.38 and Shandong Gold-Mining (600547.SH) is up 1.6% at CNY50.22. The Shenzhen index is 0.7% higher at 1193.81. (andrew.galbraith@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNH, or offshore USD/CNY, is at 6.4205, down from 6.4265 late Wednesday, following a stronger CNY fixing onshore earlier. Trading is quiet though, says a trader at a Chinese bank, adding the focus is on G-7 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the JPY amid a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  theme. "The pair will follow the direction of other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 --if the euro gains strength, the dollar/CNH will see more downward pressure." She tips the pair to trade in a 6.4190-6.4250 band. (fiona.law@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] Precious metals are mixed in volatile trade, with gold slipping in and out of positive territory on some profit-taking while investors generally remain nervous about the global economy and sovereign debt troubles. Gold is accelerating higher as demand for safe havens picks up, says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Spot gold touched a record $1,673.19/oz Wednesday. The yellow metal is hitting new record highs in at least eight major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to risk aversion and loss of confidence in global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in a note; it adds the world's largest gold ETF--\uc2 SPDR Gold Trust\uc2 --registered inflows of 18 tons Wednesday alone. Spot gold is at $1,662.10/oz, up $1, silver is at $41.82/oz, up 9 cents, while platinum is at $1,774/oz, down $5 and palladium is at $793/oz, down $1 from its previous close. (arpan.mukherjee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones]The JPY falls sharply against its counterparts after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 0100 GMT, sending the USD/JPY up sharply to a fresh intraday high at 78.47 vs 77.13, and the EUR/JPY to 112.45 up from 110.72. Dealers say the MOF sold under Y500 billion so far, though analysts say this could be only the first such \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  as any persistent JPY strength could force the government to act again in the weeks ahead. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces a tough battle in the months ahead against yen strength while the situation with the U.S. economy and possible sovereign downgrade remains uncertain, keeping pressure on the dollar," says Koji Fukaya, director of fixed income and global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . While the MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last time had the benefit of coming with a joint declaration from the G-7 economies, this time it will have to be bigger in scale to have a comparable effect, as Japan is acting alone, Fukaya says.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] The KLCI is up 0.4% at 1551.81 on bargain buying after recent declines, helped by a stronger close on Wall Street Wednesday. A local dealer says "we expect investors will view the market with a very short-term perspective and could sell on any rallies in the near term. There are a lot of issues outside the country and it may be prudent to not bet aggressively at this time." He tips immediate resistance at 1555. Market breadth is positive with 267 gainers vs 101 losers. RHB Capital (1066.KU) is up 1.2% at MYR9.22, Tenaga Nasional (5347.KU) is up 1.2% at MYR6.12 and Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) is up 0.8% at MYR3.72. Among losers, Genting (3182.KU) is down 0.2% at MYR10.82 and Maxis (6012.KU) is down 0.2% at MYR5.44. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 3 Aug 2011 22:11 EDT DJ  MARKET TALK: 10-Yr JGB Yld Breaks 1.000%; BOJ Mtg -2-\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals are trading higher on the LME as the complex benefits from China-led bargain hunting following Wednesday's selloff. Three-month copper is at $9,601.75/ton, up 0.7% from its previous settlement. "In my opinion, the drop of the last few days was over-excessive," says a Singapore-based trader. Copper ended 1.5% down Wednesday amid renewed concerns over global growth and the health of the U.S. and European economies, but the trader says he's expecting copper to remain well supported at $9,500/ton despite the macroeconomic jitters; "there's good support for the metals at current levels." Zinc is the biggest gainer on the LME so far, trading 1.2% higher at $2,405/ton; it is benefiting from technical buying that is overriding the galvanizing metal's weak fundamentals. (clementine.wallop@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 03, 2011 22:11 ET (02:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110804e784000di\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngbl