\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Capitals On Guard Against Rising  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sudeep Reddy and Peter Stein   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 962 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2011\par 01:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 No country seems to want a rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --and that poses a problem for a world economy struggling with weakening prospects for growth in the world's biggest developed nations.\par \par \uc2 In the past few days, Japanese and Swiss central banks have taken steps to restrain their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have surged amid fears about the U.S. debt ceiling and Europe's sovereign debt crisis. The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved Thursday to pump more liquidity into its market. Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its key lending rate half a percentage point to 5.75%; as a result, the lira fell 2% against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "You're seeing a more concerted effort on the part of a number of countries to manage their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time when everybody has concerns about growth," said Bruce Kasman, chief economist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 . Or, as equity strategy Peter Boockvar of Miller Tabak & Co. put it in a note to clients Thursday: "The race to debase continues."\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \uc2 In an apparent response to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and continued worries about Europe, the dollar surged Thursday against the yen and the euro.\par \par \uc2 Several of the big developed economies that let the markets set their exchange rates--the U.S., the U.K. and Japan--are counting on weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help boost their exports at a time of domestic economic weakness. "Economies that are weak would naturally want to export more. A more competitive exchange rate would be one way to enhance the competitiveness of your exports," said Barry Eichengreen, an economist at the University of California at Berkeley. But if some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are to fall, others have to rise.\par \par \uc2 Several faster-growing economies--Brazil, Canada, South Korea, Israel and South Africa among them--are watching with alarm as money flees low interest rates in the U.S., Japan and Europe and sends their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising, threatening the vitality of their export sectors.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government has been among the most aggressive. Last week, it announced what amounted to a 1% tax on investors who bet the dollar would decline again the Brazilian real.\par \par \uc2 Rising trading power China, meanwhile, keeps a firm grip on the yuan, letting it rise against the dollar but not so much that it rises against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its other trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Big moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  always spark market speculation that global governments are about to mount some concerted effort to calm the waters. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weren't a major point of discussion when finance officials from the Group of Seven economies--the U.S., Canada, the U.K., Italy, France, Germany and Japan--conferred by telephone Wednesday. The major topic was the continuing European sovereign-debt crisis. Despite that unending saga, the euro has been trading in a relatively narrow range against the U.S. dollar lately. Policy makers from other parts of the world are pressing Europeans to provide a more robust response to the euro-zone crisis and to implement already announced plans effectively.\par \par \uc2 Rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in overheating economies with rising inflation worries--as with Brazil or much of southeast Asia--are a textbook antidote to inflation; they tend to retard growth and reduce import prices. But for economies with very low inflation, such as Switzerland and Japan, the rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sparking renewed worries about inflation.\par \par \uc2 In economies where governments are pursuing austerity to reduce their heavy debt burdens--the U.S., the U.K. and Europe--the task of rescuing the economies falls more heavily on central banks and on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. "Fiscal policy is off the table," Eichengreen said. "We're in a world where central banks are in some cases the policy makers of last resort."\par \par \uc2 The new signs of weakness in the U.S. economy have sparked speculation that the Federal Reserve might launch a third round of bond-buying, known as quantitative easing. The result of printing more dollars to buy bonds--increasing the supply of dollars--would tend to push down its price, its value against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold.\par \par \uc2 While expressing frequent concern about the distressingly slow growth in the U.S. economy, U.S. officials haven't displayed any concern about the dollar or noted any signs of a disorderly decline--a prerequisite for the globally coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is so often the subject of market chatter.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen, pushing it down against the dollar, came as authorities elsewhere in Asia struggled to cope with the impact of a growing global aversion to holding the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In recent weeks, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Singapore, South Korea, Malaysia, the Philippines and Thailand have pushed to their highest levels against the dollar in more than a decade.\par \par \uc2 Sovereign-debt problems in Europe have made some investors reluctant to shift their holdings from dollars into euros. But the yen is seen as a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite Japan's weak economy and massive debt burden. The other Asian countries are attractive because they are growing, their interest rates are relatively high and markets see their exchange rates as more likely to rise than fall.\par \par \uc2 They share one trait: They're not U.S. dollars. "You're seeing this ongoing diversification away from the dollar impacting across markets," said Daniel Hui, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC Global Research.\par \par \uc2 Last week, central banks in South Korea, the Philippines and Thailand jumped into the market to sell down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to traders familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 --Marc Champion, Anjali Cordeiro and Isabella Steger contributed to this article. [ 08-04-11 1944ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e784001it\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
