\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Big Bets The Yen Will Keep Strengthening\b0\par\par\uc2 749 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 2011\par 00:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Some hedge funds buying yen on belief Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to push down yen won't work\par \par \uc2 --Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday was authorized for first \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March\par \par \uc2 --Dollar climbed to 80.25 yen but rise didn't last\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Neil Shah \par    Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While Japan is taking major steps to curb the rise of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , some bold investors are placing aggressive bets that would pay off if the yen ends up strengthening.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hedge funds are buying yen and selling dollars because they're convinced that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lacks the firepower to unilaterally push down the soaring yen, and that it can't do anything to make the U.S. dollar a more attractive investment, three \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders say. Investors believe that after flying higher Thursday, the dollar/yen exchange rate will fall again in coming days, and they're positioning themselves to profit on that movement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors have for weeks turned to countries like Japan and Switzerland to escape turbulent global markets, driving up the yen and Swiss franc and making Japanese and Swiss exports more expensive abroad. When the dollar fell to around 77 yen early Thursday, Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorized the Bank of Japan to buy dollars and sell yen in its first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since March--pushing the dollar as high as 80.25 yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the rise didn't last, with the dollar already starting to fall back down Thursday afternoon partly because big investors are betting the Bank of Japan's move won't work. That's prompting Wall Street banks--which handle these trades--to buy large amounts of insurance against potential losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We are definitely seeing 'macro' players trying to go against the Bank of Japan," says Ron DiRusso, a portfolio manager at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Concepts, a New York-based \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedge fund that manages about $8 billion. Macro hedge funds make bets on economic trends, including \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "Dollar/yen will find its way right back down" if the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader heard about a single trade during Asian hours in which about $1 billion in U.S. dollars were exchanged for yen at a price of 79 yen. In addition to trades like this, Japanese exporters, which tend to accumulate dollars and need to exchange them for yen, were "just waiting for the bank to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 " so they could sell "an enormous amount" of dollars, the trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During New York's trading hours, some investors bought options to protect themselves from--and bet in favor of--more volatility in the dollar/yen exchange rate. Prices of options, which grant holders the right to buy or sell a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at a predetermined price in the future, rise with volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wasn't the only event causing major investors to buy protection against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility--they were also spooked by the plummet in stocks and risky investments like the Australian dollar. And because hedge-fund traders also haven't been making major bets on the yen lately, "the move (by the Bank of Japan) prompted much less activity than such a move in the past," another \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trader at a major bank says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, there have been so many bets that the yen will strengthen again--and very soon--that Wall Street's biggest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -dealing banks are scrambling to protect themselves from further swings and potential losses linked to their clients' trades.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When hedge funds buy options to speculate on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , these banks--which are on the other side of such trades--need to protect themselves by purchasing options, too. This resulting demand for options is sending the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market's "fear" gauges--which are based on options prices--higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One concern that banks and investors have is that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  again, prompting another sharp spike in the dollar/yen rate. Ironically, a bigger fear is that Japanese officials will completely give up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , letting dollar/yen fall to 76 yen or lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some analysts and traders fear that could trigger automatic "buy yen" orders and force banks to buy yen to hedge risks related to previous options trades--this could cause a violent replay of March's yen surge. [ 08-04-11 1856ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e784001ia\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
