\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ BLOG/MarketBeat:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 : Expect More\b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 21:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Market Beat blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/marketbeat}}}\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 By Dave Kansas\par \par \uc2 By Vincent Cignarella\par \par \uc2 The real winners from last night's yen-selling by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  were likely the country's exporters, many of whom were sitting on dollar-sell orders at around Y80.00, and its many retail investors, who were buyers of USD/JPY below Y77 in the preceding weeks.\par \par \uc2 So, although the unilateral measure has come under subtle criticism from the U.S. Treasury and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and viewed as a wasted effort that will fail to change the long-term downward trajectory of the yen, you could argue that it has been successful from the narrow perspective of politics. It kept key constituents happy.\par \par \uc2 But over the long term, history tells us that the Ministry is just buying a wasting asset. It is stocking up on dollars that look destined to continue losing value. Without the support of other countries for its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar's initial three-yen rebound was certain not to last.\par \par \uc2 A game changer would require a shift in monetary policy and that would mean negative interest rates, a difficult shift when Japan's rates are already virtually at zero and while the BOJ is already pursuing heavy quantitative easing. The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  tried its own aggressive moves this week and has seen the same apparent temporary effect with the initial rush of exuberance out of the safe-haven Swiss franc tempered by investors' overwhelming desire for security.\par \par \uc2 There are more than a handful of traders speculating that Japans' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was designed to allow Japanese exporters the opportunity to unwind long dollar positions. There was no secret that banks order books were stocked with sell orders at and above \u165\'00\'A580.00 and that level proved strong resistance. But it is not likely that the MOF stepped in just to bail out exporters. The level of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has more to do with what Finance Minister Noda sees as proper fundamentals. His view is no doubt that with Japan showing the highest debt-to-GDP ratio of any nation, anemic growth and interest rates at zero, it simply can't be a safe haven.\par \par \uc2 So what now? Will there be a round two of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? The finance minister has drawn a line in the sand. Like the last Shogun and against all odds, Noda has staked his claim: the yen is overvalued. Period.\par \par \uc2 At least one trader at a Japanese bank thinks Japanese officials have no choice but to act again, no matter how much of a losing bet it is. There is much at stake for Noda: he dare not let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  move back to the level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so soon. The trader's opinion is that, if not tonight, certainly below the 78.00-77.50 range, they will be back.\par \par \uc2 And yet the yen looks condemned to go lower. Japan cannot go it alone and the rest of the G7 knows what we know: the decline has been orderly and not universal.\par \par \uc2 But at least Japan's exporters and its "Mrs. Watanabe" retail investors will be saying, "Domo Mr. Noda."\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-04-11 1546ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e7840014j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
