\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Equities Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 19:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par DOW DROPS MORE THAN 300 POINTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks plunge, driving the Dow Jones Industrial Average down more than 300 points, as investors appear to doubt world policy makers' ability to revive the global economy and stave off a rolling debt crisis in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US JOBLESS CLAIMS BARELY CHANGED AT 400,000 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number of people claiming new jobless benefits was little changed from the week before, pointing to persistent weakness in the U.S. labor market. Claims total 400,000, down from a revised 401,000 the week before; economists expected claims to rise to 405,000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BANK OF NY CHARGING BIG DEPOSITORS TO HOLD CASH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of New York\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the biggest U.S. custodial bank, says it will begin slapping a fee next week on customers who have vastly increased their deposit balances over the past month, citing massive dollar deposits it's received over recent weeks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB REACTIVATES ANTI-CRISIS MEASURES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet announces that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will offer a six-month tender of unlimited size next week, while traders report a re-opening of its program of government bond buying.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN ACTS TO WEAKEN YEN, BOJ EASES FURTHER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's government launches a yen-selling campaign backed up by Y10 trillion in easing measures from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as officials say they need to tame market speculation threatening a fragile economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GM POSTS LARGE PROFIT ON STRONG GLOBAL SALES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Auto maker says its 2Q profit nearly doubles to $2.5 billion, or $1.54 a share, as revenue soars, bolstered by stronger global sales and an increase in production of high-margin pickup trucks and SUVs in North America. Analysts forecast $1.20 a share. Shares drop 2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BERLUSCONI WANTS GROWTH, STABILITY PACT BY SEPT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Italian government intends to present a plan in September aimed at establishing a stability and growth pact with the country's employers' association and labor unions, Prime Minister Silvio Berlusconi says.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KRAFT FOODS\f28 \uc2 \f2  TO SPLIT INTO 2 COMPANIES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kraft is splitting in two, putting its global snacks and North American grocery businesses in separate baskets. The surprise move is Chief Executive \f28 \uc2 \f2 Irene Rosenfeld\f28 \uc2 \f2 's second bold step in as many years to transform the company. Shares up 3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOE KEEPS RATES ON HOLD AS OUTLOOK DARKENS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England keeps its key interest rate at a record low for the 30th straight month against a backdrop of slackening growth in the U.K. and gloomier prospects for the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RETAIL SALES MIXED AS SUMMER SELLING WINDS DOWN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retailers are turning in mixed sales for July, a transitional month that offers early indications of back-to-school demand amid margin-cutting summer clearance sales. Limited, Hot Topic and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Costco\f28 \uc2 \f2  top estimates but Buckle and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zumiez\f28 \uc2 \f2  miss.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EMDEON AGREES TO BE TAKEN PRIVATE FOR $2.2B \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emdeon, which provides a range of information-technology services to the health-care industry, agrees to be acquired by private-equity firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 Blackstone Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  for about $2.2 billion. Shares jump 14%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par   ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARIES ======= \par  THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par  Yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Addresses Just One Of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Many Woes \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government's decision to sell yen on global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets came as a welcome respite for the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -sensitive manufacturing sector. But the economic impact of that shot across the bow may be limited.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  AL'S EMPORIUM \par  My Economics Guru Got Ahead Of Himself \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economist Robert Aliber in January predicted the U.S. economy would grow 4.5% to 5% in 2011, the stock market would rise 15% and unemployment would fall to 7%. Now he concedes his rosy outlook was 'excessively optimistic,' Al Lewis writes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ===== U.S. MARKETS ACTION ===========\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA              down 260.66 points to 11635.78 \par NASDAQ            down 65.12 points to 2627.95 \par S&P 500           down 29.87 points to 1230.47 \par 10-year T-note    105 06/32 at 2.5197 yield \par NYMEX Crude       down $3.87 at $88.07/bbl \par Dollar/Euro       down 0.1648 at 1.41572 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-04-11 1300ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 5030 55101   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e784000r4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
