\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: US, ECB Withhold Support From  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,059 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 17:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Lack of U.S., ECB backing suggests joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  not imminent\par \par \uc2 --Trichet says \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be made on multilateral basis\par \par \uc2 --G-7 deputies in contact, but no suggestion of coordinated action\par \par \uc2 (Updates with comments by Trichet in paragraphs four and five, comments on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in paragraphs 13 to 17, analyst comments in paragraphs 21 and 22, adds background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tom Barkley and Alistair MacDonald \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's decision to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  overnight in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets drew a cool response from the U.S. and Europe, suggesting a more coordinated global effort to stem the dollar's slide isn't on the table for now.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A U.S official told Dow Jones the Obama administration didn't support the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which sought to weaken the yen to help support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The official confirmed that the U.S. stance hasn't changed from earlier in the week, when a person familiar with the matter made it clear that potential action from Japan wouldn't win the Obama administration's endorsement.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The reaction to Japan's move in Europe also wasn't positive, as European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet was critical of unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  even as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reactivated its own anti-crisis measures.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trichet said ECB has always been very clear that "such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have to be made on the basis of a multilateral consensus and decision," and that Japan's move wasn't done as part of a multilateral decision, to his knowledge.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The comments from the U.S. and Europe suggest that a more coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  involving the U.S. to boost the dollar isn't imminent, though Japan has made clear it is willing to go it alone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strong flows into safe-harbor \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  like the yen and Swiss franc, caused in large part by concerns over the debt burdens of European countries and the U.S., have prompted market responses from those governments and fueled speculation of some type of global coordinated action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Deputy officials from Group of Seven leading nations spoke by phone again Wednesday, according to a European official familiar with the discussions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But while the major economies have remained in constant contact as the sovereign-debt crisis has escalated, there hasn't been any suggestion of near-term moves by the G7, the official said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The official said it's difficult to gauge the significance of the market volatility given thin trading conditions. Since euro-zone sovereign debt troubles are viewed as the main driver behind the flight to quality, it's still mostly seen as Europe's problem to handle, the official said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, the ECB tried to do just that Thursday, re-opening its program of government bond buying for the first time in five months and announcing a six-month tender of unlimited size next week to ensure banks have enough access to liquidity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was launched Thursday that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was acting by itself, warning that he would continue to "closely watch" the market. He planned to keep in touch with officials overseas, but said he would do what he thinks is necessary to help the economy recover from the earthquake and tsunami in March.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following the tragic events that month, the G-7 joined \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on March 18 to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and stem the rising yen, vowing to cooperate further on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy as needed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But current sentiment toward Japan's concerns appears to be more in line with the previous \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last September, when the Kan administration's go-alone approach ruffled feathers abroad.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  initially sought joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at that time, but wasn't even able to secure approval from the U.S. and Europe for solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , people familiar with the situation told Dow Jones.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The September \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came as the Kan administration sought to dispel concerns that it didn't have the will to protect the economy from the rising yen. Then-Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshito Sengoku and current Administrative Vice Finance Minister Eijiro Katsu led the initiative, effectively forcing a reluctant Noda to authorize the move, they said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's action particularly frustrated Europeans, and Noda had to make painful efforts to "explain" the reasons behind the move in the following high-profile international meetings to "gain understanding" of his counterparts, they said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. largely looked the other way in September, though when asked about it the following month, a senior Treasury official said the U.S. stance remained that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should be based on market fundamentals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Wednesday, before setting out on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Noda held up the 1995 campaign to cap yen strength as a "useful reference" for the Japan's current stance. That effort, largely viewed as successful, was marked by large, less frequent bouts of yen sales in which Japan mostly acted alone but at times organized a coordinated response.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan so far Thursday is estimated to have spent between $20 billion and $30 billion on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb yen appreciation, which would top the amounts spent in March and September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts said the success of the effort could prove short-lived, especially if Japan isn't able to win the support of other G-7 countries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given the global backdrop, other countries are probably not keen on seeing their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciate, as indicated by the cut in interest rates and renewed quantitative easing from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Wednesday," said David Rea and John Higgins of Capital Economics in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was pushed up above Y80 against the yen for the first time since July 12, from Y77.13 before the Bank of Japan entered the market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen sales have continued during the day, though the dollar has settled back below Y80, considered a pain threshold for Japanese exporters. By around 11 a.m. EDT, the dollar had drifted back down to around Y78.87.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Barkley and Alistair MacDonald Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9275, tom.barkley@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Takashi Mochizuki, Takashi Nakamichi and Megumi Fujikawa in Tokyo, Todd Buell in Frankfurt, Jessica Mead in London, and Ian Talley in Washington contributed to this article. [ 08-04-11 1122ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e784000lx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
