\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ UPDATE: Yen Move Highlights  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Economic Challenge\b0\par\par\uc2 1,131 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 15:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates to add analysis, details throughout)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par --\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , easing a concerted push to tame surging yen \par --Affect of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be limited \par --Some manufacturers moving production offshore \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi, Takashi Mochizuki and Chester Dawson \par    Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan moved to tackle one of the biggest challenges facing its weakened economy Thursday with a concerted push by both the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bring down the surging yen.\par \par \uc2 But the staying power of this latest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be limited, as past moves suggest \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could resume its march higher and continue to hobble its export-driven economy. Furthermore, the yen is just one of many woes facing a Japanese economy that some economists say is key to global growth amid lackluster indicators in the U.S. and Europe.\par \par \uc2 (This story and related background material will be available on The Wall Street Journal website, WSJ.com.)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market continuously Thursday, selling an undisclosed amount of yen to keep it from reaching new highs. Traders as of Thursday afternoon local time estimated that Japan had spent just under Y1 trillion, or $12.4 billion. Though market watchers had speculated that other Group of Seven nations might join a long-expected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tokyo said it acted alone.\par \par \uc2 Bolstering the government measures, the Bank of Japan said it would pour another Y10 trillion into the economy through additional easing measures, adding to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's continuing efforts to buy financial assets including government and corporate debt to improve investor sentiment and push down rates. While not directly aimed at \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates, adding more money to an economy tends to push down its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By midday European trading the dollar had risen to about Y80 from Y77.06 in New York Wednesday. The dollar reached its post-World War II low of Y76.25 on March 17 following that month's massive earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Japan Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda hinted Thursday that the government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further if necessary. "We are still [only] halfway through, so I'll sum up the result after this is over," he said.\par \par \uc2 Some market watchers predicted a long campaign. "What we've seen is a skirmish in what could become a war of attrition," said Robert Rennie, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Westpac Banking Corp\uc2 . in Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Kazuo Hirai\uc2 , who oversees \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 .'s (SNE, 6758.TO) electronics and videogames business, cheered the move to slow the yen's sudden rise. "The fact that the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a market where the changes were so rapid is a good thing for Japanese industry as a whole," he said during a meeting with reporters. Added Hiromasa Yonekura, chairman of major business group Nippon Keidanren, "the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems slightly late, but [the government] has done well." \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main stock-market benchmark rose 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the third time in less than a year that the Japanese government has moved to address the strong yen, which makes Japan's key export sector less competitive abroad. But the yen has continued its climb despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September and a combined G-7 effort that followed the devastating March 11 earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 Demand for yen has remained strong despite Japan's massive public debt--which is about twice the size of its gross domestic product--and weak growth prospects. The Bank of Japan's emphasis on near-zero rates and a stable monetary policy make the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  look like a safe harbor, drawing investors. Meanwhile, Japanese exporters generate demand by repatriating overseas earnings.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the U.S. and Europe have slashed interest rates to stimulate growth, narrowing the spread between their rates and traditionally low-yielding Japan. That makes dollar-denominated bonds less attractive for global investors.\par \par \uc2 "I am fairly skeptical about the long-term efficacy of this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Yunosuke Ikeda, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Nomura Securities Co\uc2 . "It all depends on U.S. economic fundamentals and those show no signs of getting out of a soft patch--in fact, the U.S. economy may be in a sticky patch."\par \par \uc2 The Japanese economy faces other headwinds that even a successful \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't fix. The March 11 earthquake and tsunami led to manufacturing production cuts as well as power shortages as nuclear plants were taken offline. The Bank of Japan initially forecast 1.6% output growth for the year ending in March but reduced it to 0.4% after the quake.\par \par \uc2 For Japan Inc., those factors add to other stresses that include Tokyo's decisions to backtrack on a planned cut in the country's high corporate tax rates and to shelve negotiations on entering a new multilateral free trade pact.\par \par \uc2 Those barriers, combined with increasingly stringent environmental regulations and an aging labor pool, have been widely dubbed the Roku-Juko, or Six Woes, by business leaders.\par \par \uc2 Domestic demand is expected to rise sharply over the next six months as post-quake reconstruction moves into high gear and Japanese consumers shake off their sense of self-restraint. In some quarters, expectations for a V-shaped recovery are key to global growth. "Our call that the global economy will accelerate this summer leans heavily on a strong bounceback" from Japan, \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  said in a July 21 report.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation is particularly painful for the Japanese economy because manufacturing still makes up 20% of total economic output, just above Germany's 19% ratio and well above the 13% level in the U.S., according to United Nations data. But Japanese manufactures have become experts at adapting to yen strength by boosting productivity at home and moving more production abroad.\par \par \uc2 Sony\uc2 , for example, has sought to offset the high value of the yen by moving more of its cost base to countries where it receives large amounts of revenue, such as the U.S.\par \par \uc2 However, the appreciating yen still hurts \uc2 Sony\uc2 's competitiveness with Korean rivals such as \uc2 Samsung Electronics Co\uc2 . (SSNHY, 005930.SE) and \uc2 LG Electronics Inc\uc2 . (066570.SE). Nomura Securities economist Takahide Kiuchi projects that a rapid 10% jump in the yen during the current fiscal year through March would slash average pretax profits at Japanese manufacturers by 18%.\par \par \uc2 It has also chipped away at Japan's traditionally fat trade surpluses, once the source of great friction with its trading partners. In the March-June quarter, Japan posted a trade deficit of Y1.25 trillion, which was the second largest since 1957, according to Nomura Securities.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Takashi Mochizuki and Chester Dawson of The Wall Street Journal; takashi.nakamichi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 --Megumi Fujikawa, Tatsuo Ito and Andrew Monahan contributed to this article. [ 08-04-11 0947ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e784000gl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
