\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Equities Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 15:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP STORIES \par  \par US JOBLESS CLAIMS BARELY CHANGED AT 400,000 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number of people claiming new jobless benefits was little changed from the week before, pointing to persistent weakness in the U.S. labor market. Claims total 400,000, down from a revised 401,000 the week before; economists expected claims to rise to 405,000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GM POSTS LARGE PROFIT ON STRONG GLOBAL SALES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Auto maker says its 2Q profit nearly doubles to $2.5 billion, or $1.54 a share, as revenue soars, bolstered by stronger global sales and an increase in production of high-margin pickup trucks and SUVs in North America. Analysts forecast $1.20 a share.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ECB HOLDS KEY RATE AT 1.5% AS DEBT CRISIS SPREADS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  left its benchmark interest rate unchanged at 1.5%, as expected, as a deepening debt crisis threatens stability in Italy and Spain and recent data point to weakening economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US STOCK FUTURES PARE LOSSES AFTER JOBLESS CLAIMS DATA \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures pare some losses after better-than-expected jobless claims data. They slumped earlier on growing concerns about a slowing economy despite some upbeat corporate earnings. DJIA off 90 points.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN ACTS TO WEAKEN YEN, BOJ EASES FURTHER \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's government launches a yen-selling campaign backed up by Y10 trillion in easing measures from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as officials say they need to tame market speculation threatening a fragile economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOE KEEPS RATES ON HOLD AS OUTLOOK DARKENS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of England keeps its key interest rate at a record low for the 30th straight month against a backdrop of slackening growth in the U.K. and gloomier prospects for the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 KRAFT FOODS\f28 \uc2 \f2  TO SPLIT INTO 2 COMPANIES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares up 7% premarket as Kraft puts its global snacks and North American grocery businesses in separate baskets. The surprise move comes after Kraft became the second-largest global food company with its acquisition of Cadbury last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RETAIL SALES MIXED AS SUMMER SELLING WINDS DOWN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Retailers are turning in mixed sales for July, a transitional month that offers early indications of back-to-school demand amid margin-cutting summer clearance sales. Limited, Hot Topic and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Costco\f28 \uc2 \f2  top estimates but Buckle and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zumiez\f28 \uc2 \f2  miss.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CIGNA PROFIT RISES 39% AMID LOWER COSTS \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cigna's 2Q earnings are up at $408 million, or $1.50 a share, as the managed-care company reports strong membership growth and lower health-claims costs. Revenue increases 2.9% to $5.51 billion. Analysts forecasted earnings of $1.29 a share.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par LEAR NET UP 11% ON BROAD GROWTH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lear 2Q earnings are up at $177.5 million, or $1.65 a share, as the auto-parts supplier reports higher sales across all regions, led by North America and Asia. Net sales climb 21% to $3.68 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RIO TINTO POSTS RECORD 1H NET, RAISES BUYBACK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2 's 1H earnings surge to $7.6 billion even as it warns of sovereign-debt risk in the U.S. and Europe. The world's second-largest iron ore miner also lifts its share buyback plan to $7 billion from $5 billion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  \par  \par   ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARIES ======= \par  \par THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par Yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Addresses Just One Of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Many Woes \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese government's decision to sell yen on global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets came as a welcome respite for the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -sensitive manufacturing sector. But the economic impact of that shot across the bow may be limited.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par THE WALL STREET JOURNAL \par US Settlements With Wall Street Firms Hit Snag \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government settlements with Wall Street firms over alleged municipal-investment bid-rigging have hit a snag, as private lawyers fight for bigger payouts for their clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   ============ U.S. MARKETS ACTION =========== \par DJIA              11896.44 from Wednesday \par NASDAQ             2693.07 from Wednesday \par S&P 500            1260.34 from Wednesday \par 10-year T-note    104 21/32 at 2.5800 yield \par NYMEX Crude       down $0.89 at $91.04/bbl \par Dollar/Euro       down 0.0129 at 1.4214 \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 08-04-11 0900ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 5030 55101   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e784000cx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
