\par\par \b \uc2 US Stock Futures Swing Lower In Volatile Trading; DJIA Off 150\b0\par\par\uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 14:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Weekly jobless claims show little change to weak U.S. employment\par \par \uc2 --Stocks futures swing as ECB's Trichet says downside risks "may intensify"\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fails to lift Asian, European markets\par \par \uc2 --Kraft to split company into two publicly traded companies, shares gain\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Brendan Conway and Tomi Kilgore \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--U.S. stock futures pointed to a lower open Thursday after a data showing little change to jobless benefits claims last week failed to assuage investor concerns over a slowing economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 About an hour before the open, Dow Jones Industrial Average futures shed 135 points, or 1.1%, to 11689. The Dow had closed up 30 points on Wednesday, rallying nearly 200 points off the intraday low to halt its losing streak at eight sessions. Standard & Poor's 500 stock index futures lost 16 points, or 1%, to 1239 and Nasdaq 100 futures dropped 25 points to 2279.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Before the data, Dow futures had shed 104 points, while S&P 500 futures lost 14 and Nasdaq 100 futures dropped 22. Changes in stock futures do not always accurately predict stock moves after the opening bell.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets were primed for another volatile open. U.S. stock futures swung shortly after the data as European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet said the downside risks to growth may intensify, reflecting investor worries over the health of the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The number of initial claims for jobless benefits during the latest week fell by just 1,000 to a seasonally adjusted 400,000 in the week ended July 30, the Labor Department said Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The four-week moving average of new claims, considered a more reliable indicator of the labor market's recent performance, fell by 6,750 to 407,750. Economists generally think the economy is adding more jobs than it is shedding once the weekly claims figure falls below the 400,000 level. That hasn't happened since early April.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overnight, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stabilize the yen, which had risen as investors moved into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  considered safer assets in recent weeks. But the move to calm markets wasn't enough to distract investors from growing concerns over a slowing economy ahead of the release of key labor market data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian markets ended mostly lower, but Japan's Nikkei 225 rose slightly as the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yen's rise, giving exporters a lift. European exchanges were broadly lower, with the Europe Stoxx 600 down to its lowest level in nearly a year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar surged more than 3% to a three-week high against the yen, and also gained against the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold futures rose to about $1,670 an ounce, after reaching an all-time high in intraday trading on Wednesday. Crude oil futures slipped to $91 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In corporate news, shares of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kraft Foods\f28 \uc2 \f2  surged 7% after the blue chip company said it would split into two publicly traded companies and lifted its full-year earnings outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 General Motors gained 0.6% after reporting better-than-expected second-quarter results and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zipcar\f28 \uc2 \f2  climbed 9.1% after it projected third-quarter revenue above current forecasts.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Dendreon tumbled 63% after the company said sales of its prostate cancer drug Provenge were growing slower than expected, and that it planned to cut jobs to reduce costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, monthly same-store sales results from retailers were less than stellar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Brendan Conway, Dow Jones Newswires; (212) 416-2670; brendan.conway@dowjones.com [ 08-04-11 0852ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1106 20716 4116 5042   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020110804e784000cg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
