\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Dives On Risk-Aversion, ECB Action\b0\par\par\uc2 By Javier E. David and Tania Chen   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 23:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--A decision by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to restart buying of troubled euro-zone bonds did little to soothe investors or the euro, which hit a new record low against the Swiss franc as markets feared that Europe's long-festering debt drama could be taking a turn for the worse.\par \par \uc2 Markets were initially captivated by Japan's surprise yen-selling foray, which came without the support of other major economies. However, frenzied selling of high-yielding assets amid global sovereign-debt woes yanked support from under the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and triggered broad safe-haven buying of the dollar.\par \par \uc2 With the global growth outlook darkening, the ECB held interest rates unchanged, as most analysts expected. But its semi-dovish language -- including an acknowledgment that uncertainty had heightened considerably -- unnerved investors who interpreted it as a policy shift.\par \par \uc2 The euro zone's comparatively higher interest rates have been the sole incentive for investors to hold the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- particularly as fears grow that the problems that struck Greece, Ireland and Portugal could soon spread to Spain and Italy. As a result, the perception that the ECB could be less aggressive in tightening monetary policy hit the euro hard.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, while the ECB did purchase the debt of financially strapped euro-zone countries, some questioned why it did nothing to support the bonds of Italy and Spain, two of the euro zone's largest members and currently in the crosshairs of jittery sellers.\par \par \uc2 "Both what [the ECB] did and didn't do was interpreted badly," said Alessio de Longis, vice president and portfolio manager at Oppenheimer Funds.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action on Thursday "was a euro negative," he added. With the global economy heading for a slump and inflation seemingly declining, "there's less of a case for tightening monetary policy."\par \par \uc2 With the Dow plunging by more than 500 points in aggressive late-day selling, the beleaguered greenback reverted to its form as a safe haven, albeit of last resort. Both the Swiss franc and the yen have become the premier safety assets, despite efforts from Switzerland and Japan to undercut the strength of their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, the euro was at $1.4100 from $1.4322 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y78.90 from Y77.07, while the euro was at Y111.27 from Y110.37. The U.K. pound was at $1.6260 from $1.6424. The dollar was at CHF0.7628 from CHF0.7705. The euro plunged to a new record low in late trading at CHF1.0769.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 75.252 from about 74.044.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unexpected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to take a backseat to the anxiety that drove major stock benchmarks deep into correction territory.\par \par \uc2 The world's third-largest economy sold its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to prevent it from undercutting a fragile economic recovery. The move drew stern rebukes from both the U.S. and Europe.\par \par \uc2 However, in an environment of acute risk aversion, analysts doubted Japan's efforts would have a lasting impact. With the dollar having lost some of its safe-haven premium, investors have turned more frequently to the yen. That dynamic was likely to counteract any efforts to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- especially if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the support of other Group of Seven economies.\par \par \uc2 "This is the worst possible timing to try and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market when you have a safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which has also been under pressure," said senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Michael Woolfolk at \uc2 Bank of New York\uc2 . "It's widely known that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rarely has long-term impact and in these market conditions it's unlikely to have even short-term impact."\par \par \uc2 In support of its yen-weakening efforts, the Bank of Japan said it would pump Y10 trillion into the economy through additional easing measures. That would add to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's continuing efforts to buy financial assets, including government and corporate debt to improve investor sentiment and push down rates.\par \par \uc2 -By Javier E. David, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-4564; javier.david@dowjones.com [ 08-04-11 1709ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020110804e7840005b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
