\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(8/3) Japan Steps Up Warnings On The Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2011\par 04:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 3\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -- Japanese authorities, increasingly worried about the high value of the yen and its impact on an already weak economy, have increasingly hinted at market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is set to consider steps to pump more cash into the economy by purchasing bonds and other assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said on Tuesday that the yen is "strongly overvalued" and made it clear he has been in touch with overseas authorities on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters, fueling speculation that the government may act to stem the yen's steady rise.\par \par \uc2 "In principle, it is desirable for fundamentals to be reflected in the market, and in this sense, the yen is strongly overvalued," Mr. Noda said at a regular morning news conference. "Its movement continues to be one-sided."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, meanwhile, prepared for a meeting of its policy board on Thursday and Friday, when it is expected to consider additional purchases of assets on the open market, measures that could help to push the yen lower.\par \par \uc2 Despite Japan's lackluster economic outlook and massive government debt, the yen has emerged as a haven for investors facing growing uncertainty in the U.S. and Europe. The strong yen is worrying Japanese officials as it handicaps exporters trying to recover from disruptions caused by the March 11 earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell briefly to 76.29 yen on Monday in New York trading, putting it near its post-World War II low of 76.25 yen that was reached on March 17. It recovered some ground following the U.S. House's approval of an increase in the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 The yen's jump in late March prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to undertake joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the other Group of Seven leading industrialized nations on March 18 in the wake of the disasters.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether consultations with foreign authorities on the yen are under way, Mr. Noda said he had been communicating with "every" overseas authority on the yen.\par \par \uc2 It is natural for Japan's government to alert the U.S. in advance if it decides to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a person familiar with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy affairs said.\par \par \uc2 The person said he didn't know whether Tokyo has already won a go-ahead from U.S. Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While Mr. Noda has been warning about the problems of a strong yen for the past few weeks, signs are growing that Japanese officials are at near the end of their patience.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can stage an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at any time," another person said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, who had been skeptical that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would act, now generally concur that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is becoming increasingly likely.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities are waiting to see how the dollar would respond after the U.S. Senate's expected approval of raising the U.S. debt ceiling, which came early Wednesday Tokyo time, several people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty over the debt talk "has been a reason behind the recent selling of dollars," one person said.\par \par \uc2 Also on Japanese officials' radar screen are U.S. jobs data for July, due Friday, which would shed light on whether the dollar's recent weakness is in line with economic fundamentals, they said.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan officials have likewise become increasingly concerned that the stronger yen could weigh on Japanese business and household sentiment and prompt domestic firms to shift their business \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  overseas.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most likely step would be to expand its asset-purchase program. In an unusual program for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a 10 trillion yen fund to buy financial assets ranging from government and corporate bonds to exchange-traded funds and real-estate investment trusts.\par \par \uc2 Japanese manufacturers have been stepping up calls on the government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. There was a similar clamor for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the days before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling on Sept. 15, 2010, the first such solo action in more than six years.\par \par \uc2 Other senior officials have also weighed in. Trade Minister Banri Kaieda said on Tuesday that "the government as a whole must consider countersteps" against the yen rises.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Andrew Monahan and Megumi Fujikawa contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 02, 2011 22:16 ET (02:16 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110803e783000b3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
