\par\par \b \uc2 Q+A-Will  \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX intervention \highlight\uc2 -BOJ easing combo work?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara   \par \uc2 900 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2011\par 04:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be happy to leave the extra funds in the market if it were to step in to weaken the yen and in fact is likely to flank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with additional monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 The yen has soared nearly 5 percent in the past month, as investors dumped the U.S. dollar in favour of other liquid assets out of fear that the world's biggest economy may lose its coveted AAA credit rating because of its huge debt.\par \par \uc2 That has alarmed Japanese government officials, who have threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep yen rises from hurting the export-reliant economy just recovering from damage of the deadly March earthquake.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan is likely to ease monetary policy further this week if Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to amplify the yen-weakening effect.\par \par \uc2 HAS \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  STERILISED RECENT YEN-SELLING \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Ordinarily when a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or a government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the bank also tries to absorb, or "sterilise" the extra cash by offering securities for sale, so that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is neutral for monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Sterilisation is essentially aimed at preventing the selling of domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from creating inflation, or weakening the effect of monetary tightening if such is pursued by the monetary authorities. In Japan, which has been plagued by deflation for more than a decade, there is no such need.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo left \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilised in recent instances when it stepped into the market -- the March joint Group of Seven \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just after the earthquake and its solo action in September 2010. Its 35-trillion-yen selling spree in the 15 months to March 2004 was sterilised but effectively offset by the cash it was pumping into the banking system via its quantitative easing policy.\par \par \uc2 Throughout this period, the BOJ has kept interest rates virtually at zero and left monetary policy ultra-loose.\par \par \uc2 DOES UNSTERILISED \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WORK BETTER?\par \par \uc2 In theory, yes.\par \par \uc2 Central banks generally prefer unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they want to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and do not have a pressing inflation problem because sterilisation would negate the effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by draining the extra money from the market, pushing up short-term interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Higher short-term rates relative to those of other countries will boost the value of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and dampen the effect of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WHAT WILL THE BOJ DO THIS TIME?\par \par \uc2 The BOJ will almost certainly refrain from draining the extra liquidity created by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maximize the yen-weakening effect.\par \par \uc2 During \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003 to 2004, the BOJ never made it clear whether those \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were unsterilised even as it pumped huge amount of money into the banking system.\par \par \uc2 That has changed recently. When the G7 nations jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March, the BOJ said openly that it will leave \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilised to amplify the effect of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  action with extra yen liquidity.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also pumped record amounts of funds into the banking system via its regular market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to calm market jitters after the quake. That indirectly helped weaken the yen, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 HOW WILL THE BOJ LOOSEN POLICY THIS TIME?\par \par \uc2 Unlike during its quantitative easing in 2001-2006, the BOJ does not target current account deposits parked with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It now treats the size of a pool of funds set aside for purchases of instruments ranging from government bonds to private debt and exchange-traded equity funds as a measure of its monetary policy stance.\par \par \uc2 Any easing of monetary policy will thus take the form of adding more funds to the existing 10 trillion yen pool, rather than bolstering base money.\par \par \uc2 Still, if the Finance Ministry decides to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely pump massive amounts of money into the banking system through its market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Any such move will aim at boosting the effect of weakening the yen and stabilising financial markets.\par \par \uc2 WILL \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 /MONETARY EASING COMBO WORK?\par \par \uc2 The combination of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and BOJ monetary easing may have a symbolic effect in weakening the yen by showing Tokyo's determination to stem excessive rises in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the effect may be short-lived because Japan is unlikely to get consent from its G7 counterparts to sell yen in huge amounts like during the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2003-2004 when, unlike now, the global economy was in good shape and only Japan was suffering from a banking crisis, market players say.\par \par \uc2 Any accompanying monetary easing will be relatively small in scale too as the BOJ would increase its asset purchases by 5 trillion yen, or 10 trillion yen at the most.\par \par \uc2 The effect of flooding markets with extra cash would also be negated by the fact that Japanese markets are already awash with liquidity. The amount of current account deposits commercial banks park at the BOJ is down from post-quake records but still at around 30 trillion yen, near the peak when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopted quantitative easing in 2001-2006.\par \par \uc2 There might be a bigger psychological impact on markets if the BOJ reverts to full-blown quantitative easing or boosts purchases of long-term government bonds. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unwilling to take these steps at least for now.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Tomasz Janowski and Vidya Ranganathan)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/BOJ (Q+A)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110803e78300036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
