\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Yen sinks after  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 ; stocks near lows\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki   \par \uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2011\par 16:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 4 (Reuters) - The yen tumbled from near record highs on Monday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-damaging strength, while world stocks held above 2011 lows as expectations grew for more policy action in developed countries.\par \par \uc2 Japan spent an estimated 1 trillion yen ($13 billion) to sell the yen throughout Tokyo and London trading hours and is likely to leave extra liquidity from the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the system. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  pledged to ease monetary policy further to bolster growth by buying assets such as stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 Japan's action follows an unexpected interest rate cut by Switzerland to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 There are expectations that may be the forerunner for fresh rounds of quantitative easing by the Federal Reserve and Bank of England, while the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under pressure to resume bond buying to ease the euro zone debt crisis.\par \par \uc2 "This is going to be a long and drawn out campaign, since for Japan the Fed is more likely to cut than hike interest rates and thus the dollar remains pressured and for the Swiss there seems no resolution to the euro zone debt crisis," said Chris Turner, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 3.7 percent to 79.90 yen , having fallen as low as 76.29 on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI world equity index fell 0.6 percent, although it stayed above the previous day's 2011 low. The index is on track for its biggest weekly gain in a year.\par \par \uc2 European stocks rose 0.2 percent while emerging stocks fell 0.6 percent.\par \par \uc2 Last year's Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and easing by the Federal Reserve led to Brazil's finance minister Guido Mantega to declare a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" was erupting as developed economies sought to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support export growth.\par \par \uc2 This in turn boosted hot money inflows to emerging countries, fanning inflation and helping trigger interest rate rises by some central banks.\par \par \uc2 "It seems a fresh chapter is opening up in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars, with both Japanese and Swiss officials trying to draw lines in the sand regarding the strength in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  they are prepared to tolerate," Turner said.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil <CLc1> was steady at $91.92 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.8 percent against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The euro was down 0.6 percent at $1.4240.\par \par \uc2 EUROPE DEBT BOUNCE\par \par \uc2 There were some positive moves in Europe. Speculation that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may resume its bond buying helped Italy's 10-year government bond yield fall back below 6 percent. Bund futures <FGBLc1> were down 33 ticks.\par \par \uc2 Speculation the Federal Reserve may buy Treasuries again contributed to a turnaround on Wall Street on Wednesday as the previous round involving $600 billion bond buying which ended in June supported risky assets.\par \par \uc2 Two former top Fed officials conditionally endorsed a further round of bond buying, according to the Wall Street Journal .\par \par \uc2 Italy and Spain have been under increased pressure in recent weeks as markets feel the size of the euro zone's bailout fund is too small to protect larger fringe economies if contagion from the Greek crisis cannot be stopped.\par \par \uc2 "On the back of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets) there's improved expectation that the ECB could do something today - maybe a longer term repo or something in the secondary markets," a bond trader said.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 76.890 Japanese Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Anirban Nag)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL/ (WRAPUP 5)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110806e783000ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
