\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  hits yen, stocks fall for third day\b0\par\par\uc2 888 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2011\par 16:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells yen to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , BOJ boosts asset-buying scheme\par \par \uc2 * Nikkei rises, but global growth worries weigh on others\par \par \uc2 * ECB next in focus, eyes on its bond-buying programme\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds more comments)\par \par \uc2 By Ian Chua\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Aug 4 (Reuters) - The yen tumbled on Thursday as Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its recent gains, though gold stayed near a record high on uncertainty over whether the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would join the fray by resuming bond purchases to fight a crisis of confidence.\par \par \uc2 A day after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  unexpectedly cut rates to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen skidded around 3.0 percent against the dollar on the back of repeated yen selling by Japan, which fears a stronger yen will impede its economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan later in the day said it would ease policy further to bolster growth, pledging to buy more assets such as stocks and bonds.\par \par \uc2 The weaker yen buoyed shares of big Japanese exporters, driving the Nikkei share average up 0.2 percent, on a day when the rest of the region fell around 1.4 percent, weighed down by worries that the U.S. economy is stalling and may even be in danger of slipping back into recession.\par \par \uc2 European shares were seen likely to open higher as well, though the focus will be squarely on the outcome of the ECB's policy meeting due at 1145 GMT and ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet's news conference at 1230 GMT.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Swiss and Japanese "may increase pressure on them (the ECB) a little, and indeed on the EU too," said Christopher Gothard, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  for \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "The ECB however has been marching to its own tune of concerns about inflation pressures and we don't expect a huge turnaround in posture from them just yet."\par \par \uc2 The global economic slowdown and debt market turmoil meant the ECB would probably hit the pause button on further interest rate increases, but it might signal a readiness to buy bonds again.\par \par \uc2 Increased activity by authorities in the past 24 hours to curb gains in what were considered the safest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the G10 has made traders nervous that policymakers were cracking down simultaneously in markets.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar dropped further from a 30-year peak set on Monday after the country's finance minister said he preferred the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be weaker.\par \par \uc2 Other Asian authorities were also seen likely to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow gains in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The uncoordinated move risks competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially in Asia," said Andy Ji, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied to a high of 79.40 yen , from near 77.00 before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the euro surged to 113.36 yen , from around 110.70.\par \par \uc2 WARY\par \par \uc2 Reports of a merger between two Japanese industrial giants, \uc2 Hitachi Ltd\uc2  and \uc2 Mitsubishi Heavy Industries Ltd\par \par \uc2 , to create one of the world's largest infrastructure firms further supported Japanese stocks.\par \par \uc2 But other stock markets in the region struggled as worries about slowing global growth sapped investor enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia Pacific shares outside Japan\par \par \uc2 shed more than 1 percent, taking losses to more than 5 percent in three days.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's KOSPI was among the biggest losers, falling about 2.4 percent to well below its 200-day moving average for the first time in over a year. Foreign sellers dominated for a third session.\par \par \uc2 Markets are also awaiting results of a Spanish bond auction after yields on Spanish and Italian bonds jumped in recent sessions on fears those economies would be engulfed by debt problems.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ noted tensions in Europe's debt markets and ongoing worries about the U.S. debt problem as it eased monetary policy by boosting its asset buying scheme.\par \par \uc2 Latest U.S. figures continued to paint a sombre picture for the U.S. economy with the pace of growth in the services sector falling in July to its lowest since February 2010, while new U.S. factory orders also fell in June.\par \par \uc2 The reports followed poor figures on U.S. consumer spending and factory activity. That, along with the festering European debt crisis, is likely to keep buyers cautious.\par \par \uc2 Still, with the SNB and BOJ injecting a fresh round of liquidity into markets and talk of possibly more quantitative easing (QE) from the Fed, this could set the stage for a rebound in riskier assets in coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Former top Fed officials were quoted in a Wall Street Journal article saying the Fed should consider a third round of bond purchases if inflation slowed from recent elevated levels and if the economy continued to underperform.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think QE3 is quite on the table yet in the United States, but the market will be thinking it's possible if required. All those things do suggest we shouldn't be getting too risk negative," said Greg Gibbs, a strategist at RBS.\par \par \uc2 Among other commodities, copper climbed 0.2 percent to $9,556 a tonne, while U.S. crude <CLc1> slipped 0.4 percent to $91.57 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Swati Bhat and Jongwoo Cheon)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL/ (WRAPUP 4)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110806e783000ed\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
