\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Calls 1995  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  "Useful Reference"\b0\par\par\uc2 542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2011\par 09:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister calls 1995 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign "a useful reference"\par \par \uc2 -- Again calls the yen "strongly overvalued" and out of line with economic conditions\par \par \uc2 -- Sees the need for all-out government efforts to prevent yen rises\par \par \uc2 -- Calls on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to steer monetary policy in a way consistent with government policy\par \par \uc2 (Adds finance minister's comments in 5th, 7th, 11th paragraphs)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japan's finance minister on Wednesday called the nation's 1995 efforts to fight yen rises through various forms of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a "useful reference," suggesting he considers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  a policy option to counter the yen's current rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Just looking at the (1995) \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , there was joint \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the U.S. and Europe. There was also coordinated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the U.S. And there was solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as well," Noda said during a parliamentary session. "As such, 1995 was the year in which the government took every measure to prevent yen rises. This is a very useful reference."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda also acknowledged that policymakers at the time deployed various other tools, including monetary and fiscal policies, to weaken the yen. The dollar fell to a low of Y79.75 in April 1995, the post World War II low until March this year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda declined to say whether or how soon he may order yen-selling, but emphasized the need for the government to do everything in its power to stop the yen's gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I believe the country must make all-out efforts from the perspectives of preventing yen rises and ... preventing the hollowing out of industries," Noda said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The comments underline the growing sense of crisis about the rising yen, which takes a toll on exports. Manufacturers, ruling-camp lawmakers and newspaper editorials have all urged the government to act soon, saying the yen's strength could snuff out the nascent economic recovery from the March 11 earthquake and tsunami.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It has been said (the yen's strength) is partly due to various economic developments overseas," Noda said. "But even though some clear prospects are gradually emerging on those issues, the situation isn't changing, meaning the Japanese yen is strongly overvalued in a one-sided manner. This isn't reflecting economic fundamentals."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda appeared particularly worried about the possibility of manufacturers accelerating their shift to factories overseas to cut their exposure to exchange-rate risks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unless Japan solves problems such as the strong yen and electricity shortages, it could become "a depressed nation inhabited by only the elderly and small companies," he said. "You couldn't generate jobs in such a situation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noda also sent a signal to the Bank of Japan reminding it that it also has a responsibility to preventing such an industrial hollowing out.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think the BOJ shares common awareness of this issue," he said. "I want the BOJ to consider monetary policy in a way that's consistent with various economic policy steps to be taken by the government from now."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; +81-3-6269-2818; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Andrew Monahan and Megumi Fujikawa contributed to this story. [ 08-03-11 0321ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020110803e7830001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
