\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA DAILY  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  OUTLOOK - Majors\b0\par\par\uc2 1,890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2011\par 01:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Following are expected trading ranges and outlooks for nine major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs today:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           Immediate Range   Larger Range \par USD/JPY    76.78-77.41      76.25-77.85 \par EUR/USD   1.4258-1.4454    1.4143-1.4537 \par AUD/USD   1.0674-1.0785    1.0622-1.0825 \par NZD/USD   0.8570-0.8678    0.8534-0.8737 \par GBP/USD   1.6356-1.6475    1.6249-1.6546 \par USD/CHF   0.7629-0.7788    0.7610-0.7857 \par USD/CAD   0.9564-0.9647    0.9545-0.9667 \par EUR/JPY   109.57-111.01    109.06-112.25 \par EUR/GBP   0.8692-0.8758    0.8663-0.8769 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 (Ranges are calculated using recent high and lows, information on the placement of option strikes, and technical analysis - Fibonacci levels, trendlines and moving averages.)\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY - to consolidate with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by yen-funded carry trades amid improved investor risk sentiment (VIX fear gauge eased 5.69% to 23.38) as U.S. stocks reversed steep early-session drop to end in positive territory (DJIA up 0.25%, Nasdaq up 0.89% overnight) and snap 8-day losing streak, helped by Wall Street Journal article quoting 3 former top Federal Reserve officials signaling support for further monetary easing if inflation slows. USD/JPY also buoyed by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand; heightened expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after surprise moves by Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in strong CHF; better-than-expected U.S. ADP national employment report showing private-sector jobs increased by 114,000 in July vs +105,000 forecast. But USD/JPY topside limited by Japan exporter sales, weaker USD sentiment (ICE spot dollar index last 73.93 vs 74.53 early Wednesday) on surprise drop in U.S. ISM non-manufacturing PMI to 52.7 in July from 53.3 in June (vs 53.5 forecast), possible S&P downgrade of U.S. credit rating. Data focus: 1230 GMT U.S. July 30 weekly jobless claims. USD/JPY daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics in bullish mode. Resistance at 77.41 (yesterday's high), then at 77.85 (Tuesday's high); breach would target 78.05 (Monday's high), then 78.17 (July 27 high), 78.75 (July 26 high) and 79.03 (July 21 high). Support at 76.78 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 76.29-76.25 band (Monday's low-March 17 record low), then psychological round-numbered level of 76.00 and 75.00, then 74.54 (projected base of descending channel formed with April 2009 high of 101.45 and Nov. 2009 low of 84.81 on weekly chart).\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD - to consolidate as market await 1145 GMT ECB interest rate announcement: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to leave its key interest rate unchanged at 1.5% after having raised it twice since April. EUR/USD supported by weaker USD sentiment, stronger-than-expected euro-zone June retail sales (rose 0.9% on month vs +0.5% forecast), better-than-expected euro-zone July composite PMI of 51.1 (vs 50.8 forecast, sovereign demand for euro. But EUR/USD gains tempered by persistent worries over indebted euro-zone countries; fears over Italy's ability to finance its hefty debt despite comments from PM Berlusconi that the country is "economically and financially solid". Data focus: 1000 GMT German June manufacturing orders. EUR/USD daily chart mixed as stochastics neutral, bullish outside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 1.4454 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.4537 (July 27 reaction high), then 1.4577 (July 4 reaction high) and 1.4696 (June 7 reaction high). Support at 1.4258 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 1.4143 (yesterday's low), then 1.4133 (July 20 low), 1.4068 (July 19 low) and 1.4009 (July 18 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD - to consolidate. Supported by weaker USD sentiment, improved investor risk appetite, AUD-USD interest rate differential, sovereign demand for Aussie. But AUD/USD gains tempered by Tuesday's less-hawkish-than-expected RBA statement; disappointing Australian June retail sales and international trade data; softer commodity prices (CRB spot index closed down 4.5 yesterday at 337.32); worries over slower China economic growth; Aussie sales on soft AUD/NZD cross. Daily chart still negative-biased as MACD & stochastics in bearish mode. Resistance at 1.0785 (yesterday's high), then at 1.0825 (hourly chart); breach would expose upside to 1.0906 (previous base set Friday), then 1.1007 (Tuesday's high), 1.1065 (Monday's high) and 1.1075-1.1080 band (July 28 high-July 27 high). Support at 1.0674 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 1.0622 (100-day moving average), then 1.0556 (July 18 low), 1.0521 (July 12 low) and 1.0386 (July 27 reaction low).\par \par \uc2 NZD/USD - to consolidate. Underpinned by lower-than-expected NZ 2Q unemployment rate of 6.5% (vs 6.7% forecast), weaker USD sentiment, improved investor risk appetite, expectations of rate hikes from RBNZ as early as next month, NZD-USD yield gap, Kiwi demand on soft AUD/NZD cross. But NZD/USD gains tempered by softer commodity prices; worries over slower China growth. Daily chart still negative-biased as stochastics falling from overbought; MACD staged bearish crossover against its exponential moving average; bearish parabolic stop-and-reverse signal hit at 0.8632 yesterday. Support at 0.8570 (yesterday's low); breach would target 0.8534 (July 21 low), then 0.8521 (July 20 low) and 0.8375 (July 15 low). Resistance at 0.8678 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.8737 (hourly chart), then 0.8784 (Tuesday's high) and 0.8840 (Monday's 30-year high).\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD - to consolidate with bullish bias as markets await 1100 GMT BOE monetary policy announcement: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to keep policy rate on hold again at record low 0.5% (as it has since March 2009) and its asset purchase program unchanged at GBP200 billion. GBP/USD underpinned by surprise rise in UK CIPS services PMI to 55.4 in July from 53.9 in June (vs forecast for drop to 53.2); weaker USD sentiment; improved investor risk appetite; GBP demand on soft EUR/GBP cross. Daily chart positive-biased as stochastics reverting to bullish mode at overbought; MACD still bullish. Resistance at 1.6475 (Monday's high); breach would expose upside to 1.6546 (May 31 reaction high), then 1.6745 (April 28 top). Support at 1.6356 (hourly chart); breach would expose downside to 1.6249 (yesterday's low), then 1.6222 (Tuesday's low), 1.6191 (previous cap set July 14), 1.6120 (July 21 low) and 1.6082 (200-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 USD/CHF - to consolidate with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surprise move to rein in CHF appreciation: SNB vowed to trim 3-month interbank interest rates to as close to zero as possible and boost on-call cash deposits by an extra CHF50 billion; also warned it is keeping close eye on market developments and ready to take further measures to fight excessive CHF strength. USD/CHF also supported by CHF-funded carry trades amid improved investor risk appetite. But USD/CHF gains tempered by weaker USD sentiment. Daily chart still negative-biased as MACD bearish, while stochastics stay suppressed at oversold; though inside-day-range pattern completed yesterday. Resistance at 0.7788 (yesterday's high); breach would expose upside to 0.7857 (Tuesday's high), then 0.7954 (Monday's high), 0.8033 (Friday's high) and 0.8048 (July 28 high). Support at 0.7629 (yesterday's low), then at 0.7610 (Tuesday's record low) and psychological 0.7600; breach would expose downside to next psychological round-numbered levels of 0.7500 down to 0.7200 (projected base of descending channel formed with June 1, 2010 high of 1.1730 and Oct. 14, 2010 low of 0.9461 on weekly chart).\par \par \uc2 USD/CAD - to consolidate with risks skewed lower after hitting 3-week high of 0.9647 yesterday. Undermined by weaker USD sentiment, improved investor risk appetite. But USD/CAD downside limited by softer commodity & oil prices (Nymex crude settled down $1.86 yesterday at $91.93/bbl). Data focus: 1215 GMT Canada July official international reserves. USD/CAD daily chart still positive-biased as MACD & stochastics bullish, but latter at overbought. Support at 0.9564 (yesterday's low), then at 0.9545 (Tuesday's low); breach would expose downside to 0.9488-0.9483 band (Monday's low-Friday's low), then 0.9455 (July 28 low) and 0.9403-0.9400 band (July 26 3.5-year low-psychological level). Resistance at 0.9647 (yesterday's high); breach would target 0.9667 (July 13 high, near 55-day moving average), then 0.9778 (July 12 reaction high) and 0.9816 (200-day moving average).\par \par \uc2 EUR/JPY - to trade with risks skewed higher. Underpinned by improved investor risk sentiment; \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importer demand; expectations of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But EUR/JPY gains tempered by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  exporter sales; euro-zone sovereign debt woes. Daily chart mixed as MACD bearish, but stochastics turning bullish. Resistance at 111.01 (Tuesday's high); breach would expose upside to 112.25 (Monday's high), then 113.23 (July 27 high), 113.54-113.57 band (July 26 high-July 22 high) and 113.89 (200-day moving average). Support at 109.57 (hourly chart), then at 109.06 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to 108.73 (Monday's low), then 106.50 (March 17 bottom).\par \par \uc2 EUR/GBP - to consolidate with bearish bias after hitting 2-month low of 0.8692 yesterday. Daily chart negative-biased as MACD & stochastics bearish, although latter at oversold, suggesting sideways or lower EUR/GBP trading near-term. Support at 0.8692 (yesterday's low); breach would target 0.8663 (200-day moving average), then 0.8606 (May 26 swing low) and 0.8529 (March 10 reaction low). Resistance at 0.8758 (yesterday's high); breach would temper near-term negative outlook, targeting 0.8769 (Tuesday's high), then 0.8805 (Monday's high), 0.8847 (July 27 high) and 0.8883 (July 26 high).\par \par \uc2 -By Jerry Tan, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4046; Jerry.tan@dowjones.com [ 03-08-11 2326GMT ]\par \par \uc2 This is a financial news and information service. It is provided in general terms and does not take account of or address any individual user's position. To the extent that this article includes suggestions as to various possible investment strategies which users might consider, it does so in only general terms without reference to the personal factors which should determine any user's investment decisions. Nothing contained in this service constitutes personalized investment advice. Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors shall not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article. This article does not constitute or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security.\par \par \uc2 [ 03-08-11 2326GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1075 4033   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110803e783001fm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
