\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Time Not Right For Japanese Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 , Says BarCap\b0\par\par\uc2 420 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2011\par 11:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying time with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 -- Newly appointed Mr. Yen's reputation at stake\par \par \uc2 -- Dollar may still weaken to JPY75 against yen\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Japanese authorities may be inching closer towards \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the open market to tame the yen's strength but now might not be the right time for the Japanese to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Trying to halt the yen's gains at a time when the dollar could be facing more headwinds may not be the best of tactics, the bank said in a note. Among the factors it said would likely hold back Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of Japan in the short-term is the possibility of a U.S. credit rating downgrade and weak U.S. non-farm payrolls data, which is due to be published Friday.\par \par \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  also cited potential capital flows back into Japan in August, when Japanese investors are due to receive coupon payments and redemptions on their holdings of U.S. Treasuries, which could provide support for the yen.\par \par \uc2 Takehiko Nakao, the newly appointed Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs--a role also fondly referred to as Mr. Yen--has his reputation to consider if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fails, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said. He will want to avoid what happened in September last year when Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was followed a week later by widespread expectations of more U.S. quantitative easing, which hit the dollar and rendered the Japanese action useless.\par \par \uc2 "Thus, we think it is more likely that the MoF is trying to buy time with verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  until a more appropriate moment arrives for actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  [if needed] to maximize its effectiveness," it added.\par \par \uc2 Unilateral Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  still appears more likely than a coordinated effort with other Group of Seven industrialized nations, given that current yen strength is due to problems in the U.S. and Europe rather than due to global instability, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 That said, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said the dollar could reach still the JPY75 level against the yen in the near term due to lingering market concerns about a potential U.S. sovereign downgrade and the medium-term negative impact from fiscal tightening on U.S. growth.\par \par \uc2 At 0915 GMT the dollar was trading at JPY77.37 against the yen.\par \par \uc2 -By Siva Sithraputhran, Dow Jones Newswires, +44 020 7842 9462 (siva.sithraputhran@dowjones.com.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 02, 2011 05:28 ET (09:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020110802e782002nr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
