\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 4-Japan vows to stem yen rise, PM calls for BOJ action\b0\par\par\uc2 824 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2011\par 01:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * PM Kan wants BOJ help in supporting economy\par \par \uc2  * Noda: Will aim for maximum effect if Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  * Yen holds steady on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  jitters\par \par \uc2  * BOJ Shirakawa signals readiness to ease this week \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)\par \par \uc2  By Stanley White and Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept markets on guard for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and more monetary easing on Wednesday, with the finance minister vowing to stem the yen's rise and the prime minister calling for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to protect the economy.\par \par \uc2  The yen held broadly steady against the dollar as recent repeated jawboning by Japanese authorities kept markets wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while a deal to raise the U.S debt limit eased pressure on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  While verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached fever pitch, Prime Minister Naoto Kan called for the Bank of Japan's support a day before its two-day rate review, a sign that monetary loosening could be on the cards even if Tokyo chooses to hold off with selling yen just yet.\par \par \uc2  "Japan's economy is in the process of recovering from disaster, so we must closely watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. We want the BOJ to continue to support the economy," Kan told a regular meeting of cabinet ministers to discuss risks to the economy, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor attended.\par \par \uc2  Japanese officials fear that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's near 5 percent surge in the past month will harm the economy, which skidded into its second recession in three years following the March 11  earthquake and tsunami.\par \par \uc2  BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa signalled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's readiness to ease policy this week, saying the board will scrutinise the negative impact that recent yen gains could have on the economy.\par \par \uc2  Lingering worries that the United States faces a ratings downgrade and that Europe's debt crisis will spread further  could push the yen higher again, forcing Japan to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third time in about a year.\par \par \uc2  "I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are reflecting economic fundamentals at all," Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda told lawmakers on Wednesday, threatening possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  He declined to comment on whether Tokyo would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the timing of any such move, but warned that if Japan were to step in it would aim for maximum effect.\par \par \uc2  Swiss policymakers on Wednesday cut the target interest rate to stem gains in their rapidly rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  , which like the yen is also considered a haven in times of financial stress, and warned they would take further steps if needed.\par \par \uc2  Kan's rare request for BOJ action likely means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ease policy on Friday even if it precedes any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the finance ministry, analysts said.\par \par \uc2  The fall in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Nikkei average for a second day on Wednesday also heightened the chance of monetary easing as it could hurt business sentiment, which the BOJ focuses on in determining whether to ease. \par \par \uc2  The yen hovered around 77.16 yen to the dollar, after it soared on Monday within a hair's breadth of March's record high of 76.25 yen. Japanese government bonds jumped partly on expectations of monetary easing this week. \par \par \uc2  If the BOJ were to ease, it will likely top up its 10 trillion yen ($130 billion) asset buying scheme, under which it buys government bonds and private debt, by 5 trillion yen. Most of the increase will be in JGBs and corporate bonds given the relatively big size of these markets in Japan.\par \par \uc2  EYES ON U.S.\par \par \uc2  Moody's Investors Service said it had confirmed the United States' top AAA rating but assigned it a negative outlook after lawmakers passed a deal to raise the U.S. borrowing limit and reduce the deficit. \par \par \uc2  Despite the debt agreement, the dollar was unable to make much headway, weighed down by worries about the health of the U.S. economy following a batch of dour data.\par \par \uc2  Markets also await possible action by ratings agency \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2 , which has yet to give its opinion of the debt deal hammered out in Washington that many predict will include a cut in its top rating for the world's biggest economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been priming the markets for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the yen tested its record high, signalling it may try to tame the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a combination of yen-selling and monetary easing. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in concert with the Group of Seven in March when expectations of fund repatriation after the earthquake pushed the yen to a record high.\par \par \uc2  This time, most market players believe Japan would have to go it alone since the yen's gains are more about dollar weakness than anything else. Tokyo last acted solo in September 2010, when it sold 2.1 trillion yen.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 77.145 Japanese Yen) \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Rie Ishiguro, Editing by Tomasz Janowski)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 4) (PIX)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110805e78200270\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
