\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Fall Sharply; S&P 500 Hits 2011 Closing Low\b0\par\par\uc2 2,569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 August 2011\par 00:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      11866.62 -265.87  -2.19% \par Nasdaq     2669.24  -75.37  -2.75% \par S&P 500    1254.05  -32.89  -2.56% \par FTSE 100   5718.39  -56.04  -0.97% \par Xetra DAX  6796.75 -157.23  -2.26% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   100 3/32   +3/32 0.320%  -4.7BP \par 5Y Tsy  101 11/32  +14/32 1.222%  -9.0BP \par 10Y Tsy 104 13/32 +1 4/32 2.611% -12.9BP \par  \par USD/JPY    77.13-16  -0.08% \par Range      77.81-76.98 \par EUR/USD    1.4204-14  -0.29% \par Range      1.4282-1.4152 \par AUD/USD    1.0792-96  -1.61% \par Range      1.1006-1.0786 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.50-11.00% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    13.05-13.55% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    12.55-13.50% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,644.50   +$22.80 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,618.80-$1,647.80 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $93.79    -$1.10 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fresh worries over the state of the U.S. economy pushed stocks to the longest losing streak in nearly three years, sending the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  500-stock index to a fresh 2011 closing low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average tumbled 265.87 points, or 2.19%, to 11866.62, Tuesday. The blue-chip index's eighth consecutive decline marks its longest losing streak since October 2008. It has lost 6.7% during the skid, dating back to July 22. This is only the sixth time in more than 30 years that the Dow has dropped for eight straight sessions.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's selloff steepened just before the closing bell. The Dow dropped more than 100 points in the final hour of trading. All 30 Dow components finished in the red.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 shed 32.89 points, or 2.56%, to 1254.05, marking its seventh straight loss. The index suffered its biggest percentage drop in a year and registered the lowest close since December.\par \par \uc2 All 10 sectors in the index finished in negative territory, led lower by consumer-discretionary and industrial stocks.\par \par \uc2 The technology-oriented Nasdaq Composite gave up 75.37 points, or 2.75%, to 2669.24, its biggest percentage drop since last August.\par \par \uc2 "The market is in corrective mode," said Tom Donino, co-head of trading at \uc2 First New York Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors fretted even as President Barack Obama signed into law a bill that raises the nation's debt ceiling and cuts the budget deficit by at least $2.1 trillion over the next decade. Markets are now focused on the weak growth that is plaguing the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Data Tuesday showed Americans cut spending by the most in nearly two years and saved at a faster rate during June, signs that underscored the economy's lack of vigor.\par \par \uc2 "All the debt-ceiling drama did was mask what's really happening in the economy," said Rob Stein, global head of asset management at Astor Asset Management. "The ultimate outcome is slow growth for years and years."\par \par \uc2 In corporate news, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  was the Dow's biggest decliner, dropping 4.6% after it reported second-quarter earnings that were slightly above expectations, but revenue that fell shy of estimates.\par \par \uc2 MetroPCS Communications\uc2  lost more than one-third of its market value after the provider of prepaid wireless-phone service recorded a slowdown in subscriber growth. Shares plunged 37%. Rival Leap Wireless International, which reports quarterly results Wednesday, saw its shares drop 21%.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower Tuesday amid ongoing uncertainty about the strength of the domestic economy and sustained investor caution about the outcome of a deal to raise the U.S. debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended lower Tuesday as possible U.S. credit rating downgrades and weak manufacturing data freshened worries over the global economic recovery, with Chinese lenders leading the decline.\par \par \uc2 Investors are likely to retreat to the sidelines in anticipation of next week's release of key China economic data, including July inflation figures, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell sharply Tuesday, as strong yen jitters and a cloudy U.S. economic outlook hit machinery shares particularly hard.\par \par \uc2 "Combined with the weakness in the U.S. economy, the reason behind this stronger yen is the lack of policy from the Japanese government side," said Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities. "We don't see any decisive stance by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets."\par \par \uc2 "If the Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and implement further monetary easing, it will make it easier for investors to buy Japanese shares," said \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro.\par \par \uc2 Suzuki Motor Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Inpex Corp\uc2 . are due to report earnings Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures prices fell for a third straight day in U.S. trading, settling at the lowest level in more than a month on renewed concerns about the state of the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures pushed to record highs in U.S. trading Tuesday as concerns about a slowdown in global growth and Europe's debt crisis spurred investor demand for the metal as a refuge.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed mixed on the London Metal Exchange Tuesday as the markets continued to follow macro developments, although most metals managed to remain relatively stable amid a darkening economic picture.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The passage of the contentious U.S. debt ceiling deal by the Senate did little to alleviate persistent investor concerns Tuesday, with the Swiss franc surging to a record high against the euro and dollar as investors fretted about sovereign debt risks and a lethargic global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After weeks of heated debate, the Senate passed a bill to raise the statutory borrowing limit of the world's largest economy. The move effectively avoids a default on U.S. obligations. However, analysts questioned whether it would prove sufficient for the U.S. to avoid a downgrade of its triple-A sovereign credit rating--which could have wide repercussions on investing decisions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings Tuesday called the deal "a step in the right direction," but cautioned the government still has more work to do to maintain its pristine credit rating.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moody's Investors Service affirmed the U.S. triple-A rating after the country raised the debt ceiling, but assigned the rating a negative outlook.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In assigning the negative outlook, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  indicated, however, that there would be a risk of downgrade if there is a weakening in fiscal discipline in the coming year; if further fiscal consolidation measures aren't adopted in 2013; if the economic outlook deteriorates significantly; or if there is an appreciable rise in the U.S. government's funding costs over and above what is currently expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The meat and potatoes of the agreement are not sufficient for people to forget the process" and their aversion to risk, said David Gilmore, partner at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics in Essex, Conn."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Europe's sovereign debt turmoil is threatening the financial stability of Italy and Spain, just as major economic indicators have begun pointing downward and putting a damper on higher-yielding assets. As a result, risk-sensitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the euro have tumbled, and safe-haven assets such as the franc and gold have surged to new heights.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Political agreements don't promise solvency," Gilmore added. "The dynamics of working off debt are very poor, and markets are responding appropriately."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Enter the Swiss franc. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  falls into the category of what some analysts refer to as a 'strong balance sheet' country: low debt, marginal unemployment and attractive growth prospects. As a result, the franc is an attractive shelter for jittery traders unconvinced that the dollar and euro can overcome their economic baggage.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Both the dollar and euro fell to new life lows for the third consecutive trading session. The dollar fell to a new record low at CHF0.7642, down more than 2% on the day. Meanwhile, a broadly weaker euro also tumbled to its lowest ever at CHF1.0845.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Tuesday in New York, the euro was at $1.4200 from $1.4251 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y77.14 from Y77.21, while the euro was at Y109.52 from Y110.03. The U.K. pound was at $1.6292 from $1.6320. The dollar was at CHF0.7633 from CHF0.7835.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 74.472 from 74.262.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Benchmark yields for major government bonds in the U.S., Germany and the U.K. hit fresh annual lows Tuesday, a sign of heightened anxiety about the health of the global economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The strong demand for the safe-haven assets amid a selloff in stocks and other risky investments suggests that market participants are increasingly nervous about the pace of the global economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even though the risk of a U.S. default is now averted--President Barack Obama Tuesday signed into law a compromise bill from Congress--there's still no relief in the market. Investors are getting pummeled by myriad factors: fears about a potential economic drag from the spending cuts, the risk of a downgrade on the U.S.'s credit rating, signs of contagion in the euro zone spreading to larger economies and disappointing global data over the past several sessions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a sign that the euro zone debt crisis is getting worse, government bonds in Italy and Spain continued to sell off. The 10-year yield on Italian government debt jumped above 6% and its yield premium over German bunds widened to a fresh euro-era peak.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a result, safe-haven markets such as Treasurys, German bunds, U.K. gilts and gold all rallied. The 10-year gilt yield, which moves inversely to its price, touched a record low while the 10-year bund and Treasury yields both hit their lowest levels in more than eight months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year Treasury yield fell as low as 2.603%, the lowest level since Nov. 10--the month when the Federal Reserve launched a $600 billion Treasury bond-buying program aimed at supporting the economic recovery. The two-year Treasury yield hit a fresh record low of 0.312%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The fundamental question is about growth, job creation and the increasingly negative outlook for both for the remainder of the year," said Christian Cooper, head of U.S. dollar derivatives trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jefferies & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . [ 02-08-11 2222GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cooper said the debt deal "effectively removes the prospect of further government stimulus," and "the Federal Reserve is largely out of options beyond further purchases which will have no near term impact on job creation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anxiety about the economy was also reflected in the outperformance of long-dated Treasurys. The 30-year Treasury was the best performer as many investors shifted out of short-dated notes to grab some extra yields with the two-year note's yield trading just a touch above a record low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year yield fell below 4% for the first time since November, and the break below that level triggered more buying as some bond bears rushed to cover their wagers, betting on a further decline in price, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Tuesday afternoon trading in New York, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was 1 4/32 higher, pushing down the yield to 2.611%. The two-year note was 3/32 higher to yield 0.32%, while the 30-year bond was 3 1/32 higher to yield 3.904%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended slightly higher Tuesday after the PBOC left the yield on its one-year bills sold in the regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  unchanged for the sixth straight week, signaling it has no plans to raise the country's interest rates, even as Xinhua said a window for further tightening is approaching.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market sentiment is also being boosted by easing concerns over inflationary pressure after the Ministry of Commerce says China's wholesale pork prices in the week to July 31 fell 0.5% from a week earlier, a Beijing-based trader with a local insurer said. Pork prices, which rose 57% in June, have been a main driver of China's rising inflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Continued yen strength and slumping Japanese equities pushed safe-haven Japanese government bonds higher in Asia Tuesday, helped also by speculation that the Bank of Japan may discuss further monetary easing in a two-day meeting starting Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BOJ officials have also grown increasingly vocal about the risks posed by the higher yen, which threatens to disrupt economic recovery after the March 11 earthquake and tsunami.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could expand its asset-purchase program as one way to cushion the damage from the strong yen. Such a move would offer JGBs further support, they say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, the result of a 10-year bond auction Tuesday "confirmed that 1.1% is an OK level to buy bonds," Mizuho Securities analyst Jun Kawakami said. "I don't rule out that the 10-year yield could fall below 1.000%."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Senate Approves Bill Raising Debt Limit \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Confirms US Aaa Rating, Assigns Negative Outlook \par Obama Says Debt-Ceiling Debate 'Unsettling' To Economy \par Fitch Says US Could Get Negative Outlook \par Pimco's El-Erian: US Growth Outlook Lower \par US Hasn't Given \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Support For Possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - Source \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Hones Rhetoric, Calls China 'Assertive' \par Italy's Leaders Brace For Day Of Reckoning \par GM, Ford Report Modest Gains In New-Car Sales \par US ITC To Investigate Apple Claims Against Samsung \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  Seek Dismissal Of Trustee's Madoff Lawsuits \par Greek Bond Deal Talks 'Progressing Well' \par Geithner Says Confidence In US 'very Damaged' \par PBOC Gov: China Welcomes US Debt-Limit Raise, Will Watch Developments -Xinhua \par American \f28 \uc2 \f2 Honda Motors\f28 \uc2 \f2  July Sales Down 25.6% \par Toyota Motor USA July Total Sales Down 22.7% \par Nissan N Amer Reports Sales Up 2.7% In July \par ADB President: Yen's Strength Is Excessive -Nikkei \par Japan To Test-Drill For Methane Hydrate Off Aichi Coast -Nikkei \par Supermarkets Step Up Checks For Radioactive Beef -Nikkei \par Toyota Aims To Make Record 8.9 Million Vehicles Worldwide In '12 -Nikkei \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2315  Japan        Jul Japan Services PMI \par 2330  Australia    Jul Australian PSI \par 0130  Australia    Jun Retail Trade \par 0130  Australia    Jun International Trade in Goods & Services \par 0200  Australia    Jul VFACTS vehicle sales \par 0230  China        Jul China Services PMI \par 0230  Hong Kong    Jul Hong Kong Whole Economy PMI \par 0500  India        Jul India Services PMI \par 0750  France       Jul Services PMI \par 0755  Germany      Jul Services PMI \par 0800  EU           Jul Euro-zone Services PMI \par 0800  EU           Jul Euro-zone Comp PMI \par 0830  UK           Jul UK Official Reserves \par 0830  UK           Jul CIPS Services PMI \par 0830  UK           Jul Bank of England narrow money \par 0900  EU           Jun Retail trade \par 1100  US           Jul 29 MBA Mortgage Applications Survey \par 1215  US           Jul ADP National Employment Report \par 1300  US           Treasury's quarterly refunding announcement \par 1400  US           Jul ISM Non-Manufacturing Report \par 1400  US           Jun Manufacturers' Shipments, Inventories & Orders (M3) \par 1430  US           Jul 29 EIA Petroleum Status Report \par N/A   UK           Aug Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee meeting \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 02-08-11 2222GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110802e782001cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
