\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,130 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2011\par 12:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN'S FINANCE MINISTER UPS RHETORIC, CALLS YEN "STRONGLY OVERVALUED" \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister Tuesday called the yen "strongly overvalued" and made it clear he has been in touch with overseas authorities on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  matters, fueling speculation the government may step in to stem the yen's steady rise. (published 0556 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN FINANCE MINISTER: WON'T TALK ABOUT YEN RATE RANGE FOR \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister on Tuesday declined to comment on whether the yen is already in a range that could prompt Tokyo authorities to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. (published 2319 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UK CONSTRUCTION SECTOR ACTIVITY GROWS AT SLOWER PACE IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Activity in the U.K. construction sector grew at a marginally slower pace in July, while employment in the sector fell for the second consecutive month as confidence remained weak, a survey showed Tuesday. (published 0152 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ITALY, SPAIN BONDS FALL, SAFE HAVENS SURGE ON DEBT FEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Italian and Spanish 10-year bond yields hit fresh euro-era highs, safe-haven German bunds and U.K. gilts surged, and the euro fell Tuesday as worries about the euro-zone sovereign debt crisis and a global economic slowdown returned to rattle investors. (published 0218 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: OECD INFLATION RATE EASES FOR FIRST MONTH SINCE NOV 2010 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The annual rate of inflation across developed economies eased in June, the first decline since November 2010. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BRAZIL FOREIGN RESERVES UP $10.3B IN JULY, REACHING $346.14B \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign reserves totaled $346.14 billion in July, up from $335.77 billion at the end of June. (published 0307 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: EURO-ZONE PRODUCER PRICES WERE FLAT IN JUNE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices of goods leaving factories in the 17 countries that use the euro were unchanged in June, indicating that inflationary pressures may be easing. (published 0210 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par GERMAN GOVT ADVISER: EURO-ZONE CRISIS WILL RETURN BY SEP -REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe's sovereign-debt crisis isn't over and financial-market tensions are likely to resurface next month, Lars Feld, an economic adviser to the German government, said in an interview published Tuesday. (published 2351 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par OECD: NEW AID DEAL BUYS TIME, BUT GREECE MUST STEP UP REFORMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greece has gained time to implement its ambitious fiscal and economic reforms because of the latest aid deal from international creditors, the OECD said Tuesday, but cut its growth forecast for the country and warned of a long recovery ahead. (published 0239 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US, EUROPE DEBT UPHEAVALS SEAL RBA ON-HOLD RATES VERDICT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Debt woes in the U.S. and Europe sealed a decision Tuesday by Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep interest rates steady in August, and economists now say policy will be left unchanged until early 2012. (published 0540 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par MOODY'S CREDIT OFFICERS IN TOKYO FOR MEETINGS WITH JAPAN OFFICIALS-SOURCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A delegation of credit rating officers from Moody's Investors Service is visiting Tokyo for talks with Japanese officials as it prepares to conclude a review on the country's sovereign debt rating for a possible downgrade, people familiar with the matter said Tuesday. (published 0303 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US HOUSE VOTES 269-161 TO PASS DEBT CEILING INCREASE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. House members voted to increase the debt ceiling and cut $2.1 trillion from the budget deficit Monday evening, sending a historic blueprint for the nation's fiscal future on to the Senate as lawmakers sought to avert an economic disaster the day before the nation could begin defaulting on some obligations. (published 2318 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BANK OF CHINA LAUNCHES YUAN CASH BUSINESS IN ZAMBIA; EYES WIDER AFRICAN ROLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of China Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., the country's fourth-largest lender by assets, said Tuesday it launched yuan cash business services in Zambia with the aim of boosting economic ties between China and Africa. (published 0147 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par XINHUA: WINDOW AROUND AUG 10 FOR POSSIBLE CHINA RATE HIKE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A key window for a possible rate increase in China is around Aug. 10 and the country's current rate hike cycle will likely be over if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't move at that time, Xinhua news agency said. (published 0242 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BOJ WATCH: POLICY BOARD TO DISCUSS STEPS AGAINST STRONG YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan will likely discuss additional monetary easing steps to counter the negative impact of a strong yen on the economy when its policy board meets later this week, and such measures could come in tandem with the government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. (published 0016 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par EURO PLUMS NEW LOWS AGAINST SWISS FRANC AS DEBT FEARS HEIGHTEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders Tuesday pulled out of risky \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bets and sent the euro to a fresh all-time low against the Swiss franc as euro-zone debt concerns intensified. (published 0039 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESUMES PRESSURE ON U.S. TO RESOLVE DEBT PROBLEMS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China stepped up its public pressure on the U.S. Tuesday, calling for a resolution of long-term fiscal challenges even as the House passed a agreement to raise the country's debt ceiling to avert a potential government default. (published 0347 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par DATA SNAP: SPAIN JULY JOBLESS CLAIMS -1% ON MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spanish jobless claims fell in July for the fourth consecutive month, as summer hiring in the tourism industry provided the country's ailing job market with some seasonal relief. (published 0000 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA NEEDS TO BE MORE VIGILANT AGAINST FURTHER MONETARY TIGHTENING - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China ought to be more vigilant against issuing further monetary tightening measures, the China Securities Journal said Tuesday in an editorial, highlighting that manufacturing activity is slowing to a level close to a contractionary mode. (published 2342 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA RESEARCHER: SHOULD EXPAND SCALE OF SOVEREIGN WEALTH FUNDS, INVEST IN INDUSTRY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China should expand the scale of its sovereign wealth funds and switch from financial investment to industrial investment to protect its massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, a state researcher said Tuesday. (published 2341 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par S KOREA TO ADD 4 FOOD ITEMS TO TARIFF-CUT LIST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The South Korean government will add four new food items to its list of imports eligible for tariff cuts to help stabilize consumer prices, the Ministry of Strategy and Finance said Tuesday. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN CORPORATES FACE US$548B OF DEBT REFINANCING THROUGH 2015 -S&P \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's corporate sector is facing some US$548 billion of debt refinancing through to the end of 2015, 99% of which is from investment grade-rated entities, according to analysis conducted by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (published 0001 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par  \par PREVIEW: MALAYSIA JUNE EXPORTS LIKELY UP 6.0% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The year-on-year growth in Malaysia's exports likely remained weak in June as commodity prices eased while demand from advanced economies slowed, economists say. (published 0419 GMT) [ 02-08-11 1059GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020110802e782000xn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
