\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Shares Seen Lower, Economy Worry Trumps Debt Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 4,993 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2011\par 07:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen slightly lower, core government debt little changed; \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in ranges; spot gold, oil futures up slightly\par \par \uc2 -US House Passes Debt Deal, Now Goes To Senate, Passage Likely\par \par \uc2 -US Plans Borrowing $331 Bln In July Through September -Treasury\par \par \uc2 -EU Juncker: Will Do Everything To Ensure Euro-Zone Stability-Report\par \par \uc2 -IMF: EU Basel III Proposals "A Disappointment"\par \par \uc2 -Netanyahu Ready For Talks On US Peace Plan -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 E.ON\uc2  Opens Arbitration Process In Talks With \uc2 Gazprom\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 E.ON\uc2  Eyes Going Back On Dividend Level Pledge -Report\par \par \uc2 -HearUSA Announces Agreement For Sale Of Assets To \uc2 Siemens\par \par \uc2 -RWE Considers Selling Some Distribution Units -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 AT&T\uc2  Sells Its Telmex Shares To \uc2 America Movil\uc2  For $1.37B\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Macarthur Coal\uc2  CEO Says Peabody, Acelor Bid Not Hostile\par \par \uc2 -Kirin To Buy $2.5 Billion Stake In Brazil Drink Maker\par \par \uc2 Watch for (GMT): 0830 UK Jul CIPS Construction PMI; 0900 EU Jun PPI; 1230 US Jun Personal Income & Outlays\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Asian markets tumbled on Tuesday as news that U.S. lawmakers had voted in favor of a plan to avoid a default took a backseat to renewed fears over the global economy and a possible U.S. credit rating cut.\par \par \uc2 U.S. House members voted to increase the debt ceiling and cut $2.1 trillion from the budget deficit Monday evening, sending a historic blueprint for the nation's fiscal future on to the Senate, which is expected to pass it Tuesday. Lawmakers sought to avert an economic disaster the day before the nation could begin defaulting on some obligations.\par \par \uc2 But investors will look to more U.S. economic data Tuesday, especially personal income and spending, to gauge whether consumers will continue to provide enough support to the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 European investors, facing similar concerns, are likely to lead the region's stock markets to a slightly lower start, with core debt markets little changed. The dollar and euro are little changed, with oil futures and spot gold up very slightly.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 With the U.S. debt deal almost done, investors will switch their focus Tuesday to the euro zone's own debt problems as well as the recent deterioration in economic data, likely leaving stocks a bit lower at the beginning.\par \par \uc2 For Tuesday's opening, IG Markets is calling the FTSE down one at 5773, the DAX off 14 at 6940, and the CAC off nine at 3579.\par \par \uc2 With the U.S. economy seemingly slowing down, momentum in China and Europe also appears to be decelerating. Views differ on whether expansion will pick up later in the year in all of those regions, leaving a degree on uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 A senior European official is seeking to circumvent fresh concerns about fiscally troubled euro-zone countries.\par \par \uc2 The euro zone will do what is needed to ensure financial stability, including adjusting the agreement reached at the July 21 emergency summit, the chairman of the group of euro-zone finance ministers said in a newspaper interview to be published Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "In the long term, Europe will turn out to have been a safe investment," Jean-Claude Juncker is quoted as saying by French daily Le Figaro in an advance copy of the interview late Monday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are lower on Tuesday, after markets fell but still managed to finish above session lows, as weak manufacturing data and worries of potential downgrades to the U.S. credit rating overpowered investor relief over the weekend's debt-ceiling deal.\par \par \uc2 The loss of momentum in hiring could be reflected in the July employment report Friday, said Julia Coronado, an economist with \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "Clearly, the report raises worries that the sector that has been a key engine of growth during the current recovery is losing steam, reflecting a weakening around the globe in manufacturing," she added.\par \par \uc2 "Wall Street doesn't work well with this kind of uncertainty," whether in the form of weak economic data or the state of the U.S. debt negotiations, said Alan Valdes, director of floor trading at DME Securities.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar garnered brief support as U.S. House members voted to pass the debt-ceiling deal and on safe-haven demand, amid the poor U.S. manufacturing data and continuing worries rating agencies could still downgrade the U.S.'s triple-A credit status.\par \par \uc2 "If concerns retreat somewhat over the debt ceiling issue, that's good for the dollar, but then the question is what comes next as the focus shifts to U.S. economic indicators and even the sovereign woes in Spain and other European peripherals," said Sumino Kamei, senior analyst at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ.\par \par \uc2 Major pairs Tuesday toward the European opening were little changed. On Monday, the euro fell sharply after an 11th-hour deal to avert a downgrade and default on U.S. government obligations boosted the dollar and shifted attention back to the euro zone's festering debt issues.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the temporary alleviation of fears about the U.S.'s fiscal health gave investors the opportunity to look at Europe with fresh eyes. The euro's price action suggested that investors were becoming more concerned that European Union leaders had fallen short in their efforts at a July summit to increase certainty about Greece's sovereign debt woes.\par \par \uc2 "Peripheral bond markets are distressed," said Jessica Hoversen, fixed-income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 MF Global\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "From a broader perspective it suggests the EU summit did nothing to stem contagion" to Italy and Spain, she added. "While it seems like a very distant topic given that we've been focusing on the debt ceiling, (European debt is) still an issue."\par \par \uc2 The muscular yen has put traders on alert for the possibility that Japan could enter markets and sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to squelch its strength. The Nikkei reported early Tuesday in Japan that the U.S. and Japanese governments held emergency talks by phone over the weekend, and the U.S. is open to the possibility that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own.\par \par \uc2 Greg Anderson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , said Japanese authorities might be "tempted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," but "whether they can turn off the market all by themselves seem very dubious, and it seems unlikely that (foreign) firms will help as they did in March."\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts are likely to start little changed Tuesday, while investors study sagging economic data and the euro zone's debt difficulties.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  advised investors to go long on 10-year bunds, saying the next leg of peripheral stress will be driven by further rating actions, Italian and Spanish funding concerns and the implementation of the Greek bailout package. The broker said "drastic policy making action, such as common bonds, is unlikely before Italian and Spanish 10-year yields reach 7%."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasurys are little changed Tuesday, after the market showed Monday that the frustrating pace politicians took to reach a budget agreement is no match for the anxiety created by a stalling U.S. recovery.\par \par \uc2 Treasury yields plummeted to new 2011 lows after a surprisingly weak U.S. manufacturing report dropped to its lowest point in two years.\par \par \uc2 Many traders looked for a sigh of relief once a U.S. debt solution was finally agreed upon. Some thought Treasurys would sell off because of the greater certainty, while others expected a buying rally because spending cuts as part of a deal would dent a still-struggling economy.\par \par \uc2 John Stopford, head of fixed-income at \uc2 Investec Asset Management\uc2  in London, said, "the consensus is the headline number (of the debt plan) is reasonably large but not big enough in the medium term," he said.\par \par \uc2 Ratings agency \uc2 Standard & Poor's Corp\uc2 . has said that a deal would have to start at around $4 trillion for the U.S. to maintain its top triple-A credit rating.\par \par \uc2 "The (U.S. credit) rating is the key longer term, but the deal they have falls into a grey area in terms of how the rating agencies are likely to react, and we're unlikely to get a quick response (from the rating agencies)," Charles Diebel, rates strategist at Lloyds Bank Corporate Markets, said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Treasury cut its borrowing estimate for the third quarter by $74 billion as spending grew less than expected.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department said it expects to issue $331 billion in new debt during the July to September period, $74 billion less than the $405 billion estimated in May. The agency cited lower outlays and more cash than expected, but did not provide further details.\par \par \uc2 In Tokyo Tuesday, lead September Japanese government bond futures were up 0.24 at 142.02 points after touching 142.05, their highest since Nov. 17, buoyed by the Nikkei's losses and Monday's gains in U.S. Treasurys. The latest 10-year auction went as expected.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Crude prices inched higher in Asia Tuesday with markets taking a breather after volatile trade a day earlier as bearish U.S. manufacturing data quashed initial euphoria over a debt deal, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for September delivery, gained 40 cents to $95.29 a barrel. Brent North Sea crude for delivery in September rose 16 cents to $116.97 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Serene Lim, oil and gas analyst for ANZ bank in Singapore, observed: "I think it's trading very sideways today after very choppy trading last night...markets are taking a pause."\par \par \uc2 Nymex crude will likely fall to $90 a barrel this week as the U.S. economy proves to be in worse condition than thought, said Jim Ritterbusch, president of Ritterbusch & Associates.\par \par \uc2 Traders likely will also shift their focus away from the U.S. debt issues once a deal is passed, he added.\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Three-month zinc is the only contract in negative territory on the London Metal Exchange despite three consecutive days of stock draws from London Metal Exchange-approved warehouses. Zinc is at $2,451 a metric ton, down $10 from its previous settlement. [ 02-08-11 0501GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  is recommending its clients be underweight the base metals index in their portfolios in the near-term following an aggressive move higher on the London Metal Exchange in July, traditionally a slow time of year for consumers.\par \par \uc2 Barrick Gold Ltd. (ABX) remains bullish on the outlook for gold prices and expects prices for copper to remain well supported for several years due to rising demand, a regional executive for the world's largest gold producer said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Three-month copper is at $9,703/ton, up $53.50 from its previous close and 3.5% higher than at the start of July. Spot gold is at $1,621 a troy ounce, up 70 cents from its previous close.\par \par \uc2 Gold seems to be supported by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases as prices failed to pull back as sharply as expected, a Hong Kong-based trader said.\par \par \uc2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 02-08-11 0501GMT ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12132.49  -10.75  -0.09% \par Nasdaq     2744.61  -11.77  -0.43% \par S&P 500    1286.94   -5.34  -0.41% \par FTSE 100   5774.43  -40.76  -0.70% \par Xetra DAX  6953.98 -204.79  -2.86% \par CAC40      3588.05  -83.23  -2.27% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9838.10  -126.91 -1.27% \par Hang Seng    22508.39 -154.98 -0.68% \par S&P/ASX 200   4436.70  -61.10 -1.36% \par Taiwan Index  8595.46 -105.92 -1.22% \par S.Korea Kospi 2129.15  -43.16 -1.99% \par Shanghai Comp 2660.20  -43.58 -1.61% \par Bombay SenSex 18186.88 -127.45 -0.70% \par Dow Future    12007.00 -49.00  -0.4% \par NASDAQ Future 2337.75  -8.25   -0.3% \par S&P Future    1276.60  -3.10   -0.4% \par Above are as of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      77.46-48         +0.34% \par Range        77.81    77.20 \par EUR/USD      1.4245-48        -0.02% \par Range        1.4281   1.4242 \par AUD/USD      1.0966-70        -0.03% \par Range        1.1006   1.0966 \par GBP/USD      1.6302-08        +0.06% \par Range        1.6322   1.6292 \par USD/CHF      0.7828-33        -0.06% \par Range        0.7855   0.7826 \par EUR/GBP      0.8736-39        -0.08% \par Range        0.8753   0.8737 \par \u203\'00\'CBUR/CHF      1.1154-62        -0.07% \par Range        1.1201   1.1152 \par Above are as of 0350 GMT vs NY close \par  \par USD/JPY Vol Option Contract 10.36%/11.04% \par Day earlier: 10.61%/11.29% \par EUR/USD Vol Option Contract 12.74%/13.36% \par Day earlier: 12.14%/12.76% \par AUD/USD Vol Option Contract 12.05%/12.45% \