\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei falls on US woes, but  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  risk lends support\b0\par\par\uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2011\par 01:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Next support level seen at 9,750\par \par \uc2  * U.S. economic data eyed this week-analysts\par \par \uc2  * \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  skids on weak forecast, Nomura downgrade\par \par \uc2  * Honda outperforms on unexpected quarterly profit\par \par \uc2  * Kirin outperforms on Brazilian M&A news \par \par \uc2  By Ayai Tomisawa\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 2 (Reuters) - The Nikkei average fell 1.2 percent after weak U.S. manufacturing data fanned fears about the health of the global economy and pushed the dollar lower, although the heightened risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by Japanese authorities lent support.  \par \par \uc2 Data showed the U.S. manufacturing sector grew at the slowest pace in two years in July, following similarly weak reports from much of Asia and Europe. This has made investors particularly jittery, especially ahead of U.S. unemployment data on Friday.\par \par \uc2  "Investors are concerned about the yen's rise but what  worries them more is whether demand for global cyclical companies' goods will stay intact or not," said Masanaga Kono,  chief strategist at Amundi \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  primed markets on Tuesday for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the yen tested record highs overnight, signalling it may try to tame the unit with a combination of yen-selling and easier \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  "If there is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, sentiment for companies may be lifted," said Fumiyuki Takahashi, managing director at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  He added that as long as the dollar trades around 77-78 yen, companies would probably not cut their full-year outlooks as most have assumed a rate of 80 yen to the dollar and could fill the gap with cost cuts.\par \par \uc2  "But if the yen's weakness is short-lived and the dollar trades below 77 yen again, we may not be able to avoid a sell-off," he said.\par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei finished at 9,844.59, erasing most of the gains it made the previous day. The next support level is seen at 9,750 , which is seen as a potential strike price for Nikkei 225 options.\par \par \uc2  The broader Topix index fell 0.9 percent to 843.96.\par \par \uc2  The dollar last traded at 77.25 yen.\par \par \uc2  Chip-related stocks underperformed as earnings underscored fears that a recovery in the chipmaking equipment market might come later than expected.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  fell 6.2 percent to 3,940 yen after the world's No.2 supplier of chipmaking equipment cut its annual forecast by half on Monday, hit by slowing investment by makers of chips used in PCs, smartphones and tablets. Nomura Securities cut its rating to "neutral" from "buy".\par \par \uc2  The same fears also hit \uc2 Dainippon Screen Manufacturing\par \par \uc2 which dropped 6.2 percent to 579 yen after Nomura similarly lowered its rating on the chipmaking equipment producer to "neutral" from "buy".\par \par \uc2  But Kirin Holdings Co outperformed the market, falling only 0.3 percent to 1,148 yen after it said will spend  $2.6 billion to take a controlling stake in major Brazilian beer and soft drinks maker Schincariol to expand its market share in the fast-growing South American economy.  \par \par \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  was down 0.5 percent at 3,110 yen, outperforming the market after reporting an unexpected quarterly profit and raised its annual outlook by more than a third, as Japan's No.3 automaker rebounded quickly from a severe parts shortage due to the March 11 earthquake. \par \par \uc2  But Nomura Securities, which cut its rating to "neutral" from "buy", said the company's stock is no longer considered cheap due to increasing marketing costs in the U.S. market.\par \par \uc2  Volume was thin, with 1.6 billion shares changing hands on the Tokyo stock exchange's main board, slightly lower than last week's daily average of 1.7 billion yen. \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-STOCKS (UPDATE 5)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020110801e781001te\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
