\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Fall In Volatile Trade\b0\par\par\uc2 2,711 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2011\par 00:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      12132.49  -10.75  -0.09% \par Nasdaq     2744.61  -11.77  -0.43% \par S&P 500    1286.94   -5.34  -0.41% \par FTSE 100   5774.43  -40.76  -0.70% \par Xetra DAX  6953.98 -204.79  -2.86% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 0/32  -1/32 0.375% +1.2BP \par 5Y Tsy 100 27/32  +7/32 1.325% -4.8BP \par 10Y Tsy 103 5/32 +14/32 2.753% -5.2BP \par  \par USD/JPY    77.21-24  +0.03% \par Range      78.04-76.30 \par EUR/USD    1.4250-52  -0.92% \par Range      1.4452-1.4186 \par AUD/USD    1.0970-74  -0.14% \par Range      1.1064-1.0924 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol 10.75/11.05% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol 12.89/13.31% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol 11.85/12.35% \par  \par Comex Gold        $1,621.70    -$9.50 \par Comex Gold Range  $1,608.20-$1,634.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $94.89    -$0.81 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks fell but finished above session lows, as weak manufacturing data and worries of potential downgrades to the U.S. credit rating overpowered investor relief over the weekend's debt-ceiling deal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average shed 10.75 points, or 0.09%, to 12132.49, for its seventh-straight losing session, the longest losing streak in more than a year. It was a volatile session that saw the Dow briefly fall below 12000 for the first time since late June. The blue-chip index had risen as much as 139.18 points immediately after the open, as investors expressed relief at the compromise hammered out late Sunday to raise the U.S. debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 But stocks turned negative after a reading on manufacturing in July showed barely any growth, renewing worries about the health of the U.S. economy. The Dow fell by as much as 145.16 points midsession.\par \par \uc2 The action followed the index's biggest weekly point decline since May 2010, which came last week as investors fretted that Washington had run out of time to raise the debt ceiling and stave off a potential default.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index lost 5.34 points, or 0.41%, to 1286.94, for the sixth straight decline. All sectors except utilities and telecommunications lost ground. The Nasdaq Composite shed 11.77 points, or 0.43%, to 2744.61, its fifth drop in the last six sessions.\par \par \uc2 The weak manufacturing data sat uneasily with investors who have been expecting stronger growth in the second half. Meanwhile, markets continued to eye the chance of a U.S. credit-rating downgrade, which many investors considered to be possible despite the weekend debt pact.\par \par \uc2 "Wall Street doesn't work well with this kind of uncertainty," whether in the form of weak economic data or the state of the U.S. debt negotiations, said Alan Valdes, director of floor trading at DME Securities.\par \par \uc2 Health-care stocks were the S&P 500's weakest performers after the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services said it will cut payment rates to skilled nursing facilities by 11.1% next fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Sun Healthcare Group\uc2  plunged 52%, while Skilled Healthcare Group dropped 43%. \uc2 Kindred Healthcare\uc2  slumped 29%, while Sunrise Senior Living lost 3%.\par \par \uc2 Both Merck and \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  shed 2%, leading blue-chip decliners.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended flat with an upward bias Monday as investors reacted with cautious optimism following a deal to raise the U.S. debt ceiling, while macroeconomic indicators continued to show signs of economic vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 "It's worries about inflation that are pushing stocks lower," said Jacky Zhang, an analyst at Capital Securities.\par \par \uc2 In a report published Monday, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said 64% of respondents to a survey expected inflation to continue to rise in China in August. China's consumer price index rose to a three-year high of 6.4% in June. July CPI data is scheduled to be issued next week.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares ended higher Monday, tracking gains in Asian bourses, after the U.S. Congress made progress on resolving that country's debt-ceiling impasse.\par \par \uc2 The immediate resistance for the index will be around 22,800. There are many major technical resistances at that level, including the July 2 HSI peak of 22,835, and the 250-day moving average of 22,872, Core Pacific-Yamaichi said.\par \par \uc2 "The short-term momentum is strong as the market is already oversold," it added.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose sharply Monday after U.S. President Barack Obama said congressional leaders had reached an agreement to lift the U.S. debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 "We don't know yet how the ratings agencies will respond to the U.S. debt agreement," said Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \uc2 Mito Securities\uc2 . "Once the initial market enthusiasm dies down, there is a possibility that investors will once again focus on the weaker-than-expected U.S. GDP figures."\par \par \uc2 Tuesday, \uc2 Toyota Motor Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Sumitomo Corp\uc2 . are scheduled to release earnings.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures fell in U.S. trading Monday in a broad market reversal as weak U.S. manufacturing data reignited concerns about slowing growth in the world's largest oil consumer.\par \par \uc2 Comex gold futures fell Monday as the U.S. debt-limit deal reached over the weekend eased investor demand for a safe haven, but the weaker-than-expected reading on U.S. manufacturing limited losses.\par \par \uc2 The ISM data also caused copper to reverse earlier gains on the London Metal Exchange Monday, with the metal settling at its lowest closing price in three weeks. Tin was the sole gainer, closing 0.02% higher.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro fell sharply Monday, after an 11th-hour deal to avert a downgrade and default on U.S. government obligations boosted the dollar and shifted attention back to the euro zone's festering debt issues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early in the session, markets cheered news that after weeks of heated debate the White House and congressional leaders were able to reach agreement on raising the nation's $14.3 trillion borrowing limit. The deal, which still must be voted on in both the House and Senate, must still withstand the scrutiny of ratings companies threatening to downgrade the U.S.'s triple-A credit status.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet weak U.S. manufacturing data ruined the budding optimism. News that the Institute for Supply Management's Manufacturing Index fell to its lowest level in two years undermined appetite for higher-yielding assets. With markets pricing in a resolution of the U.S. debt-ceiling debate, the poor data encouraged investors to buy the dollar anew as a safe haven from a weakening economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said the temporary alleviation of fears about the U.S.'s fiscal health gave investors the opportunity to look at Europe with fresh eyes. The euro's price action suggested that investors were becoming more concerned that European Union leaders had fallen short in their efforts at a July summit to create certainty about Greece's sovereign debt woes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Peripheral bond markets are distressed," said Jessica Hoversen, fixed-income and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "From a broader perspective it suggests the EU summit did nothing to stem contagion" to Italy and Spain, she added. "While it seems like a very distant topic given that we've been focusing on the debt ceiling, [European debt is] still an issue."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Monday in New York, the euro was at $1.4250 from $1.4397 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y77.18 from Y76.75, while the euro was at Y110.01 from Y110.46. The U.K. pound was at $1.6320 from $1.6414. The dollar was at CHF0.7835 from CHF0.7859.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the U.S. dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 74.329 from about 73.897.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a reflection that all is still not well with investors, both the dollar and euro fell to record lows against the franc as markets searched for a suitable safe-haven.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The muscular yen has put traders on alert for the possibility that Japan could enter markets and sell its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in order to squelch its strength. The Nikkei reported early Tuesday in Japan that the U.S. and Japanese governments held emergency talks by phone over the weekend, and the U.S. is open to the possibility that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on its own.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar bounced off its intraday low of Y76.29--just pips away from the all-time low of Y76.25 in March in response to the Nikkei headlines. While analysts say Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is a possibility, they doubt such a move will be coordinated, as in March when all the Group of Seven countries sold yen in an unprecedented move.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greg Anderson\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 , said Japanese authorities might be "tempted to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," but "whether they can turn off the market all by themselves seems very dubious, and it seems unlikely that [foreign] firms will help as they did in March."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasurys market showed Monday that the frustrating pace politicians took to reach a budget agreement is no match for the anxiety created by a stalling U.S. recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury yields plummeted to new 2011 lows after a surprisingly weak U.S. manufacturing report dropped to its lowest point in two years. The Institute for Supply Management showed its purchasing managers' index sinking to 50.9 in July from 55.3 in June.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The report sent investors scurrying into safe-haven Treasurys, breaking out of an indecisive morning when they mulled news that U.S. lawmakers were close to pushing through a budget deal in time to avoid a default.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The weak U.S. ISM report "put an exclamation point...on the fact that the U.S. economy is still in a precarious situation," said John Canavan, fixed-income analyst at Stone & McCarthy. He said he sees 10-year yields dropping to as low as 2.55% in the next two months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many traders thought there would be a sigh of relief once a U.S. debt solution was finally agreed upon. Some thought Treasurys would sell off because there would be more certainty, while others expected a buying rally because spending cuts as part of a deal would dent a still-struggling economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Instead, Treasurys traded in a cautious range after news of the announced agreement, as investors awaited more details. "There's a healthy skepticism," said Marc Ostwald, strategist at Monument Securities. "Markets are saying there's a deal, but prove it. We want to see the votes come through." [ 01-08-11 2210GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders were also waiting for another shoe to drop--wondering how ratings firms would interpret the deal. The current agreement is for $2.4 trillion in deficit reductions, and Standard and Poor's previously said it would downgrade the U.S. if Congress cut anything less than $4 trillion. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , meanwhile, reiterated its Friday statement saying it wouldn't downgrade the U.S. as long as the debt ceiling is lifted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The thing to worry about is really the potential for downgrade, which looks inevitable at this point," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Scott Mather\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of global portfolio management at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pacific Investment Management Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., which runs the world's largest bond fund. Mather added that U.S. Treasurys may benefit from investors' flight to liquidity, but said it is hard to see 10-year yields below the 2.5% mark.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pause in the debt impasse, for now, prompted investors to focus on the stalling U.S. economic recovery. The ISM data combined with last week's worse-than-expected second-quarter economic growth number and the fragile housing market. Monday's report "rams home the point that it is the economic fundamentals that really matter and not concerns over America's credit rating," said analysts at Capital Economics. They expect 10-year yields to fall to 2.5% by the end of the year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until a debt-deal is signed, slight pressure remains on the short end of the U.S. credit market. The Fed funds rate--the rate banks charge one another for overnight loans--leapt to 0.1500% from 0.0750%, the highest level since late March, according to CQG. That remains well within the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's targeted 0% to 0.25% range, but banks' efforts to stay liquid amid U.S. budget uncertainties are putting upward pressure on such borrowing costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In late Monday afternoon trading, benchmark 10-year notes gained 14/32 in price to yield 2.753%, while 30-year bonds rose 24/32 to yield 4.089%. Both yields are the lowest since November.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond futures ended slightly lower Monday, as the deal on the U.S. debt ceiling weakened the yen, which in turn eased concerns over the export-dependant Japanese economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JGB market participants will now focus on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's sale of Y2.2 trillion worth of 10-year JGBs Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS Securities chief rates strategist Akito Fukunaga expects the auction to create a dip-buying opportunity for investors, saying that while the U.S. debt deal provides support to risky assets, ensuing yield rises will likely be only short-lived.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Caution in financial markets is unlikely to ease, as the outlook for external demand--which should be a driver for growth after the domestic economy recovers from the earthquake--isn't very bright and there remain risks that European sovereign debt fears may revive in September and December," he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds end slightly higher Monday amid easing worries of further tightening measures after both the official and \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  PMIs showed China's manufacturing activity continued to slow in July, a Shanghai-based trader with a local fund said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's latest remarks on inflation have limited bond prices' upward momentum, he added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The PBOC will continue implementing its "prudent" monetary policy as inflation expectations in China remain high and any policy loosening could cause price pressures to rebound, it said in a statement issued after a seminar for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among events Tuesday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  is expected to leave rates on hold, according to a survey of 19 economists by Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par House Begins Debate As Deadline Nears \par Gen Re, AIG Execs' Convictions Thrown Out \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  Cuts 30,000 Jobs To Save $3.5B \par US Construction Spending Up; Manufacturing Slows \par Twitter Announces 'Significant' Funding Round \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 America Movil\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plans Offer For Remaining Telmex Stake \par Euro-Zone Manufacturing At Near Standstill \par Peabody, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Arcelormittal\f28 \uc2 \f2  Go Hostile For \f28 \uc2 \f2 Macarthur Coal\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Japan Gears Up For Emergency Action On Yen -Nikkei \par US Open To \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan Forex Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -Nikkei \par Jana Partners, Ontario Teachers Up Joint Stake In McGraw-Hill \par Bank Of Korea Buys Gold For First Time In 13 Years; Reserves $311.03 Bln End-July \par Habib Confirms Plans To Build JAC Motors Factory In Brazil \par Japan To End Discount Mortgage Rate 3 Months Early -Nikkei \par Nissan, Toyota Revamping Production At Home -Nikkei \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kansai Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  Faces Procurement Shortfall, Worsening Shortage -Nikkei \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2245  New Zealand  2Q  Quarterly Employment Survey \par 2245  New Zealand  2Q  Labour Cost Index (Salary and Wages Rates) \par 2350  Japan        Jul Monetary Base \par 0130  Australia    2Q  House Price Indexes: Eight Capital Cities \par 0130  Australia    Jun Building Approvals \par 0430  Australia    Aug \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  cash rate decision \par 0630  Australia    Jul Commodity Price Index \par 0830  UK           Jul CIPS Construction PMI \par 0900  EU           Jun PPI \par 1000  France       Jun OECD CPI \par 1145  US           Jul 30 ICSC-\f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Chain Store Sales Index \par 1230  US           June Personal Income & Outlays \par 1255  US           Jul 30 Johnson Redbook Retail Sales Index \par 1330  Sing